[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335732 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37af32ac-6d56-59b7-af88-a83bd549c119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333502} {:answer/id #uuid "89b6e43c-5175-58b9-a703-c4d3bd3ea434" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334213} {:answer/id #uuid "ec91d17d-8a59-5d87-b344-af638420e8b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335062} {:answer/id #uuid "f164ca79-a714-5ace-a91e-f41a09d97456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335104} {:answer/id #uuid "fd455317-5a16-56c0-bbb9-11acb365385d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec91d17d-8a59-5d87-b344-af638420e8b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335062} :answer-field/id #uuid "91f964f4-328c-5d44-bbab-649563165d63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe9f4359-a5d3-51ab-bfaf-67dd4916cffb" :question/math-academy-id "q-64666" :question/problem "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(t)$ given that its derivative ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the corresponding unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$ are as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k,\\,{T}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2}\\,i-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the derivative of the curve ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the magnitudes of ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the curvature is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335735 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d900898-f644-59cb-8bd9-292f8cf96161" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333108} {:answer/id #uuid "128843bf-c0d0-5b0b-9af7-5c0891c02535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333155} {:answer/id #uuid "867d9b75-2324-5dfe-830f-942376886bea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334183} {:answer/id #uuid "de2d463a-06e0-5a98-b913-8a3a09131403" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334926} {:answer/id #uuid "f58082e0-41ed-5179-bfb1-b391fbd71bba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "867d9b75-2324-5dfe-830f-942376886bea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334183} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4eb5247-b68b-5a82-8180-4c0ffe3de0aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335575}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9c7086f-08af-5d07-8de2-8cc3da9eb077" :question/math-academy-id "q-64681" :question/problem "![](images/94/94b8650466747f6858b30adbb8962e6c91159466b570f536d4999e2b08c31b67.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{1}{2},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/cb/cb569d4b7b76699259d2167b8c930e636e3fb794b14ef25a4d909c224235d7c3.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335738 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07056c3e-506d-5641-9888-b4b318e25eff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333058} {:answer/id #uuid "a034f5c0-3b14-5e29-8def-edc95efbd80e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334397} {:answer/id #uuid "cb74b243-d73b-5089-bae4-f981f0a60cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334760} {:answer/id #uuid "d2ffa90f-9b1f-53a0-9ae4-72f9fe2ebb09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334836} {:answer/id #uuid "d8504233-ad25-5121-8f69-909d3fab6b8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07056c3e-506d-5641-9888-b4b318e25eff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333058} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b238c5f-814a-5a46-a972-3a25a21b520c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8585ed26-d09c-5c8e-84bf-c2d2c03840ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-64730" :question/problem "![](images/2f/2f01b946a5c7c0f93c2171379603c4426913af5a15d0bc03434785144cfe4102.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ what is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{2}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. Therefore $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nUsing the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we finally get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335739 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "369d7dc4-1f2c-58c7-8955-91cabe185acd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333492} {:answer/id #uuid "6cbb8b9b-d6a9-5997-bce5-90424ff1180f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333979} {:answer/id #uuid "b24b6fea-adc3-54a3-94dd-ad481dc00180" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334555} {:answer/id #uuid "e0405203-826a-52d8-8a79-dcf3a04354bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334940} {:answer/id #uuid "fa731375-053c-5708-bf47-16a574de2b6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "369d7dc4-1f2c-58c7-8955-91cabe185acd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333492} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bc84e1e-1ce0-5ed5-b29d-dc063985cbbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a6d0a779-de60-5fed-8e29-8de76943c6c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-64732" :question/problem "Consider a curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s.$ Find the radius of curvature of $r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that  its  unit tangent vector $T(s)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\,k\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature: \n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{κ(s)}=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right))i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\right)k \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right))\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\,j+0\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}[-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\,j] \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right))}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right))}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)+{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}=\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{2}{9}\\right)}=\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335740 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13837c57-fcf6-59ed-a939-a470108db450" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333161} {:answer/id #uuid "66df2533-6415-54e6-aecd-02e34294c2d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333928} {:answer/id #uuid "9199da41-115a-549a-926f-7560fc8fae17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334288} {:answer/id #uuid "af3ba4c9-eaee-5458-a599-2c97ee46fb5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334534} {:answer/id #uuid "e32c6e4d-94ff-5ce6-869c-2bf3aead8861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334966}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13837c57-fcf6-59ed-a939-a470108db450" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333161} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ac7cd9a-bd4e-577f-9519-5227a1ee21af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "85d6e3bb-1239-5725-994c-6f104c6ddc40" :question/math-academy-id "q-64738" :question/problem "Consider a curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s.$ Find the radius of curvature of $r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that  its  unit tangent vector $T(s)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =⟨-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right),\\,-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right),\\,\\frac{3}{5}⟩\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature: \n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{κ(s)}=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right),\\,-\\frac{4}{5}(-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right)),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right),\\,\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{25}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right),\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\frac{8}{25}\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right))}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right))}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{25}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right)+{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}=\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{8}{25}\\right)}=\\frac{25}{8}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335742 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27d52570-d8d7-54ae-bc8f-38fedf640c57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333355} {:answer/id #uuid "2a41eb50-84fb-5e15-a2fd-b9f0d84548ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333385} {:answer/id #uuid "39155515-2930-5a3e-a663-927e2c1264f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333521} {:answer/id #uuid "96b44be0-4346-5231-9bd4-850b8f4d067b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334331} {:answer/id #uuid "f4ed2baa-d15c-547d-9c15-7f620031c5f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335136}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27d52570-d8d7-54ae-bc8f-38fedf640c57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333355} :answer-field/id #uuid "f380bd19-6826-5ceb-a952-d12a4fb5fc55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "71c558ef-3d89-5d4c-b532-d4626f624683" :question/math-academy-id "q-64761" :question/problem "Using the cross product curvature formula, find the curvature of $r(t)=⟨\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}},\\,{e}^{t}⟩$ at the point where $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}},\\,{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨-\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{1-{0}^{2}}},\\,{e}^{0}⟩=⟨0,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{{(1-{t}^{2})}^{3/2}},\\,{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨-\\frac{1}{(1-{0}^{2})^{3/2}},\\,{e}^{0}⟩=⟨-1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(0)$ and ${r}^{″}(0)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨0,\\,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{(1)^{3}}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335763 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a7c30e6-c4fd-5b9f-8b33-63bfdc242a7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333536} {:answer/id #uuid "47032272-9a8f-5bdd-9811-23fd93f7bd48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333645} {:answer/id #uuid "4b0ba869-1963-5543-916a-61f21bdfe6e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333678} {:answer/id #uuid "879c0d1e-5384-5707-b993-66e2366ac32c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334189} {:answer/id #uuid "fd0f9487-b82f-52b2-9b68-320c61641358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a7c30e6-c4fd-5b9f-8b33-63bfdc242a7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333536} :answer-field/id #uuid "20586bf7-c2fd-57ac-825e-1691e50c888b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335299}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "146a5e9e-6c97-5732-9c84-59f28f9f2729" :question/math-academy-id "q-64925" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335764 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "159bd76d-aabf-5059-a127-408d2989f0bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333182} {:answer/id #uuid "3789cd72-89a6-5e2f-9492-d7d751156542" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333500} {:answer/id #uuid "8a31df51-1d7c-5c28-b360-dd1de5d8eca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334222} {:answer/id #uuid "9424ba9f-fb60-5bfc-bff0-89eec5a0bbb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334309} {:answer/id #uuid "ac9fdbb5-b41a-5bc4-9f39-5f38dcbfbb58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a31df51-1d7c-5c28-b360-dd1de5d8eca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334222} :answer-field/id #uuid "f148a5e1-b96d-5dcb-b87b-4d3a4113df1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d34a3ba-689d-51af-888e-1a275d337823" :question/math-academy-id "q-64927" :question/problem "$cos⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $cos⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335766 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ce3c790-303b-59b5-85dc-f2d962fc4aed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333413} {:answer/id #uuid "306c0753-0f8f-5219-acd5-a6ccfd9b653a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333437} {:answer/id #uuid "36371df4-8fae-57bc-a6a0-c710e01f2699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333489} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d73050-8611-5e60-84b7-8c9e3d91e46b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334468} {:answer/id #uuid "afb5bd23-7730-589b-8ecd-14c76c5ddc83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36371df4-8fae-57bc-a6a0-c710e01f2699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333489} :answer-field/id #uuid "7734d8f6-b8d5-54fa-95d9-d19c42fbc9c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e9ce1113-6c60-54a0-96ff-819d6f8a12a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-64957" :question/problem "Find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ of the equation \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-4x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=12\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\\,x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x={e}^{t},$ we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x={e}^{t},$ we note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}=x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}_{\\text{product rule}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}}_{\\text{chain rule}}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+6y=12t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation with constant coefficients and polynomial forcing. \n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{2t}+B{e}^{3t}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t):$\n\n$$\n0-5\\alpha +6(\\alpha t+\\beta )=12t\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n6\\alpha t+(-5\\alpha +6\\beta )=12t.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6\\alpha &=12\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of }t) \\\\ -5\\alpha +6\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHence, $\\alpha =2$ and $\\beta =\\frac{5}{3},$ and so the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2t+\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, returning to the variable $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335768 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c9a56e0-5972-5209-9d5c-ea16e82e995f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333100} {:answer/id #uuid "66686a01-21cc-56d7-951a-bacb93258c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333921} {:answer/id #uuid "79013707-fe29-522d-ac8d-ac18b46d1880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334075} {:answer/id #uuid "989e0c9d-3b24-5671-9e42-b86c26764caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334342} {:answer/id #uuid "b3886518-c45d-5987-8dfe-39a52ed04936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66686a01-21cc-56d7-951a-bacb93258c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333921} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a4e3654-a019-5e30-92b9-ed3bae770d8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335446}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f6c71da-f5b0-5c03-871d-e07fb324730c" :question/math-academy-id "q-64968" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P$ with $y$-coordinate $0$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\sqrt{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+\\frac{\\pi }{4})$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $y=0$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =\\sqrt{2}(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335778 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b822d1-8ee0-58a2-93ff-cb187dfa5db0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333039} {:answer/id #uuid "172c2164-8a29-5506-a361-8ccd4decba98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{21}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333196} {:answer/id #uuid "5025acf5-7673-5024-a61f-a855686d95e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333720} {:answer/id #uuid "d263b596-7ef8-56b5-a591-3fd063e528a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334827} {:answer/id #uuid "e6630e6d-1da4-555c-a3f5-d70e2b45b53e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{21}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5025acf5-7673-5024-a61f-a855686d95e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333720} :answer-field/id #uuid "9047e8c0-5f50-5691-b54b-073e8bf98b88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "efddc82f-972e-5912-af23-4395e0d8ee20" :question/math-academy-id "q-65031" :question/problem "The point $A$ is located in the first quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{2}{5}.$ What is the $y$-coordinate of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $A$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{2}{5}$ into the above and solving for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{25}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1-\\frac{4}{25} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{21}{25} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{\\frac{21}{25}} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $y$ must be positive, so we select the positive answer. Therefore, our final answer is $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335782 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02fd9752-fd71-5807-992a-c9286c555b0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})-8x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333013} {:answer/id #uuid "22e0392e-161b-5cd0-b69d-623ba2c626ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333308} {:answer/id #uuid "3b03ca54-ca5c-5716-8df5-99f49e870d95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})-4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333544} {:answer/id #uuid "c17f6285-6a31-5a6f-ba18-48d38055ae48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})-4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334667} {:answer/id #uuid "c71294f2-657c-5001-8ad3-1983771b0d4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})+8{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334718}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c17f6285-6a31-5a6f-ba18-48d38055ae48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})-4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334667} :answer-field/id #uuid "0de89994-6248-5a37-be20-8e39be4cecaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "435d066b-0de3-5e62-8775-943aacab7cbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-65058" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y),$ calculate $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}$ given that $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})+2x\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}))\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})-4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335783 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ebb8345-8448-5af3-a7b5-f875fc57e26d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{55}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333119} {:answer/id #uuid "87f8bdf1-b693-5449-95dc-41601ab98dee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334192} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd4a426-2676-5c7e-b66d-c6dd8478bed9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{56}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334369} {:answer/id #uuid "ac5680a5-b135-5e11-a025-14cbaf19a181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334503} {:answer/id #uuid "af99c88e-1b1e-54e6-8e94-cc6dcf9705d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{56}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac5680a5-b135-5e11-a025-14cbaf19a181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334503} :answer-field/id #uuid "2831461c-74ea-58e4-8c19-3e42bd31b998" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43fa1511-b2d2-52bf-92cb-53a58f4a78b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-65059" :question/problem "![](images/5d/5d292a3598346bd05511c9c8979ba42845a88afe75a4740d8c4bdb9aa3968c87.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{5}{9},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/48/488ff32187c9a9a335e3782fb717242d899f622c43010662e28edcabe0e1bde3.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{5}{9}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{9}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{9}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{5}{9}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{25}{81}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{56}{81}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335784 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "008964b2-7dcd-5496-839e-a3936e4e413e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(x+y)({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}){e}^{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332992} {:answer/id #uuid "1991441f-3378-5cd3-80d6-8a348297f979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}){e}^{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333223} {:answer/id #uuid "2acd9894-aa37-521b-bdcd-0495b3e44ae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y(1-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}){e}^{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333394} {:answer/id #uuid "328c82a6-f437-5253-8973-23de51cae015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy(1-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}){e}^{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333457} {:answer/id #uuid "f33659c2-346a-5855-916f-d4e8545fee46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4xy(1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}){e}^{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f33659c2-346a-5855-916f-d4e8545fee46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4xy(1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}){e}^{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335121} :answer-field/id #uuid "0452e7e5-3a94-51af-af99-33d429c743a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a96b5224-3589-5ee6-9e32-9c7fe293ceb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-65060" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y),$ calculate $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}$ given that $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x{y}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =2x\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =2x[2y\\cdot {e}^{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}+{y}^{2}\\cdot ({e}^{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\cdot 2y{x}^{2})] \\\\  & =4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}+4{x}^{3}{y}^{3}{e}^{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =4xy(1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}){e}^{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335785 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "596dbc8f-087c-5cf2-accd-e0290a3590a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333808} {:answer/id #uuid "ad4202f9-1df8-5702-aa30-7ebfe4130dbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334517} {:answer/id #uuid "c29b3ce4-5742-5c0e-a80c-c46e912aa977" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334679} {:answer/id #uuid "cc5de6f1-fb22-59a3-a646-77d3c44a4d2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334771} {:answer/id #uuid "d1f58498-935a-53c3-af47-c294d082ad34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1f58498-935a-53c3-af47-c294d082ad34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334822} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e139d51-01cf-5ad5-af15-a0777e8b7fe3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ee81fb0a-2d3c-5307-89ae-7908973bbefc" :question/math-academy-id "q-65062" :question/problem "![](images/43/43629ebfaa2f9811135ffb15f5e5a59b796b83e3a525b4ab480efb9840d40879.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $sec⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f5/f5219a363cdf18cfb628148ca7b9bbe6015b521799ed4487fa9b56843eaa3043.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{3}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{6}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}.$\n\nKnowing that $sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335786 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ffeba9c-70a6-5328-9dc3-5020e0313ad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333595} {:answer/id #uuid "4eafe18b-8c02-5577-a960-94ba6f971ec7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333711} {:answer/id #uuid "7213fed3-dd70-59f5-9da6-eb714e406490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334021} {:answer/id #uuid "9dc0b081-37ac-56aa-8ceb-99b894130c9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334379} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb4516a-8d82-5f18-ae27-7aaf1618cf06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ffeba9c-70a6-5328-9dc3-5020e0313ad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333595} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0192dd7-c731-5b69-8d4c-3c9bab7026de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335562}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "555c1e17-369e-580d-8f06-901f476b3eee" :question/math-academy-id "q-65064" :question/problem "![](images/45/455df038e550d78fc29463c1a2e22dd44838d24af7428a824f9046fecc1ac1db.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/44/44a555d222e3f6ff85208e8722a728ad240c558de7982d2a480bf9ddfb23ac6f.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{15}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{10}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}\\right)}=\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335788 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0548138d-e543-5a54-a872-c6505ae35f31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4y}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333031} {:answer/id #uuid "3dd21545-2656-5f34-8899-9e63ce0f1a99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333573} {:answer/id #uuid "56f12986-c08d-5855-9c32-e38ca3128af3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333777} {:answer/id #uuid "7279d9a1-e1cd-5962-b045-74040fa8edd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y}{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334026} {:answer/id #uuid "810185ca-f52f-5e5b-a85b-df54ad11e6a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4+y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0548138d-e543-5a54-a872-c6505ae35f31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4y}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333031} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7a6c17f-ff74-5dd2-941a-c73839c7e4a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab0f3803-2840-52ec-8174-767389c2fcc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-65071" :question/problem "Calculate ${f}_{yxx}(x,y)$ given that $f(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the first-order derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${f}_{yx}(x,y)$ by differentiating ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})) \\\\  & =2y\\cdot \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${f}_{yxx}(x,y)$ by differentiating ${f}_{yx}(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yxx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{yx}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{4y}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot x-4y\\cdot 1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4y}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335789 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "194eaee8-54cb-563d-8f6d-d34f552da442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333221} {:answer/id #uuid "8dcc27a4-b6ff-5196-bfc9-478bbd73a589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334253} {:answer/id #uuid "a96bc338-b51a-5af0-adad-0e96975b2300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334473} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3570c1-3d28-5add-bb53-9c8895491a2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334516} {:answer/id #uuid "e6417934-5eda-53e4-82d9-b2e434d37de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad3570c1-3d28-5add-bb53-9c8895491a2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334516} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfce3d7a-f48e-534b-a3f3-76cd7412d6ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335631}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7581029a-054b-5d43-92f5-a2b561001b1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-65072" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e666ddc8a8eda452abd833e963446c96de5d34dfbf76e9c92564e85a48080524.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{3}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $tan⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/fc/fc291f1fc5c4a712d3eef7ea2872de621855669d97445b24a2bc7a9da55949d9.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{3}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{4}{5}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335790 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b84d4ec-9f5f-5a47-8517-3bb8f8c9e6f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333094} {:answer/id #uuid "0f97daea-fc44-5b9a-b6c8-8ae1ec14226d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{17}}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333127} {:answer/id #uuid "26887548-0fc3-51f2-9193-efc1f3262179" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333343} {:answer/id #uuid "9f0d2d95-0c48-5abf-8954-df6ad2d3263b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334390} {:answer/id #uuid "c3781df0-cce1-500d-ae05-5955c50dfe45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3781df0-cce1-500d-ae05-5955c50dfe45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334685} :answer-field/id #uuid "360fb473-e8de-57ee-bab2-0cdf46873b28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5aa7dee2-18db-5dfd-b251-d711e6634ce2" :question/math-academy-id "q-65075" :question/problem "![](images/d8/d846227996dd5fc1d6c3ca30108f548ac0d3743ec9262db0a551d28a32386996.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $cot⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/52/5284ae5a30a5d370d52c887cc6222ee4991a98d087dc751cbcfe5d7307ca1d47.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{17}{81}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{64}{81}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{8}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{8}{9}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $cot⁡\\theta =\\frac{cos⁡\\theta }{sin⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ncot⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{8}{9}\\right)}=\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{8}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335802 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f09aafd-c1e5-5dd1-bf03-b73681599bb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451477} {:answer/id #uuid "2f54d447-a39c-55bb-850f-c236243803eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f09aafd-c1e5-5dd1-bf03-b73681599bb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451477} :answer-field/id #uuid "85c75200-01ed-54ca-9326-1eca1bb956f5" :answer-field/key "The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452586} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf599bcb-83ed-5f4a-a06d-a9db97c5fe77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451479} {:answer/id #uuid "d1ec1810-7d26-56cd-aaac-4334475ae5ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1ec1810-7d26-56cd-aaac-4334475ae5ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451480} :answer-field/id #uuid "a87ea30f-e244-5f86-bffc-4a5395ff286d" :answer-field/key "The limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{2}$ along the path $y=-x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452587} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca681fe9-ee18-536a-9299-af74510e99ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451481} {:answer/id #uuid "2d5393a0-13ec-5e60-9584-47126e03236e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca681fe9-ee18-536a-9299-af74510e99ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451481} :answer-field/id #uuid "118478a6-d6eb-5b08-b5c7-fcce661c9509" :answer-field/key "The limit exists and is equal to $0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ccd0f4b9-0dbd-51e2-a95c-14471c65ba52" :question/math-academy-id "q-65145" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}=\\frac{2\\cdot 0+0}{3\\cdot 0+0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$” is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x+x}{3x+x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3x}{4x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{2}$ along the path $y=-x$” is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-2x+x}{3x-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-x}{2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit exists and is equal to $0$” is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335806 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a3d5eca-f411-5643-aa4d-35d3ef9848ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sin}^{2}⁡x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333230} {:answer/id #uuid "a457f79d-88df-50d4-b7cb-695bebb93a96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334432} {:answer/id #uuid "ad464370-15d9-5b5e-9b9e-04ae6f9aa325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334519} {:answer/id #uuid "f00a7b47-2966-5b0e-b022-de10e6ccf06c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sin}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335093} {:answer/id #uuid "fe0e56a4-9096-568a-a480-e3cc1f380f3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f00a7b47-2966-5b0e-b022-de10e6ccf06c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sin}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335093} :answer-field/id #uuid "230bd128-0f74-509e-8b76-16eab4cc9809" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335302}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f45c9a7-af57-5bcc-a401-894d9ca48316" :question/math-academy-id "q-65214" :question/problem "Simplify the expression ${cos}^{3}⁡x({sec}^{3}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{3}⁡x({sec}^{3}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x) & ={cos}^{3}⁡x\\cdot {sec}^{3}⁡x-{cos}^{3}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the identity $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ to further simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{3}⁡x\\cdot {sec}^{3}⁡x-{cos}^{3}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & ={cos}^{3}⁡x\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right)}^{3}-{cos}^{3}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & ={cos}^{3}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{3}⁡x}-{cos}^{3}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{3}⁡x}{{cos}^{3}⁡x}-\\frac{{cos}^{3}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{cos}^{3}⁡x}}{\\cancel{{cos}^{3}⁡x}}-\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the identity ${sin}^{2}⁡x=1-{cos}^{2}⁡x$ to rewrite the expression on the right-hand side.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{3}⁡x({sec}^{3}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)={sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335822 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39c4f7c1-2cc1-57ed-a18b-bf91992e00d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451483} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e4c2de-8568-5ff2-baf8-40d15c25b5ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3e4c2de-8568-5ff2-baf8-40d15c25b5ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451484} :answer-field/id #uuid "99c60c75-d597-5852-8b29-5420ba46e80b" :answer-field/key "${f}_{x}(0,1)$is defined" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452589} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96e8d67b-c006-521e-ad4e-1b3b5e5bbae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451485} {:answer/id #uuid "5fcff0a4-c6d8-5ff7-9344-ecba3634e8b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96e8d67b-c006-521e-ad4e-1b3b5e5bbae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451485} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf8a887b-1bb0-5b36-af98-54f46b4ff833" :answer-field/key "${f}_{y}(1,0)$ is defined" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452590} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bd09d87-8f09-5958-88e2-49043d0205b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451487} {:answer/id #uuid "72089ef2-d948-531c-b1f3-ac84e9dc1b0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72089ef2-d948-531c-b1f3-ac84e9dc1b0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451488} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d4fe7e1-7135-5cca-a3a0-20d39be1b879" :answer-field/key "Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1,1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b22bd39f-49d5-56c7-8797-705e83876e55" :question/math-academy-id "q-65255" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=xy{e}^{x/y}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy{e}^{x/y}) \\\\  & =y{e}^{x/y}+xy{e}^{x/y}\\left(\\frac{1}{y}\\right) \\\\  & =y{e}^{x/y}+x{e}^{x/y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy{e}^{x/y}) \\\\  & =x{e}^{x/y}+xy{e}^{x/y}\\left(-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =x{e}^{x/y}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}{e}^{x/y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement “${f}_{x}(0,1)$is defined” is true. Indeed, the function $f$ itself is defined at $(0,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,1)=0\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{0/1}=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{x}$ is also defined at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1)=1\\cdot {e}^{0/1}+0\\cdot {e}^{0/1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${f}_{y}(1,0)$ is defined” is false. If we substitute $(1,0)$ into ${f}_{y}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{y}(1,0)=1\\cdot {e}^{1/0}-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{0}\\cdot {e}^{1/0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\n- Statement “Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1,1)$” is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,1)=1\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{1/1}=e,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =1\\cdot {e}^{1/1}+1\\cdot {e}^{1/1}=2e \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,1) & =1\\cdot {e}^{1/1}-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{1}\\cdot {e}^{1/1}=e-e=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${f}_{x}(0,1)$is defined”; “Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1,1)$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335834 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b7e71a1-540f-5903-a353-483cfa6ced0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333552} {:answer/id #uuid "573aace2-d7ed-5759-b52b-c897e1f38e81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333785} {:answer/id #uuid "5af8090f-e014-5587-901a-3c106f54a0c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333821} {:answer/id #uuid "a99a4457-c774-5779-bb5c-5a973ede85f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334476} {:answer/id #uuid "b486a69e-1f42-5339-a393-a6f36f33f348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334570}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a99a4457-c774-5779-bb5c-5a973ede85f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334476} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9707a27-b3b6-57dc-891d-4427cb25a436" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "585e18d7-2aa5-5d3f-86ad-587ffce85678" :question/math-academy-id "q-65427" :question/problem "What is the exact value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-{105}^{∘})?$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $-{105}^{∘}={30}^{∘}-{135}^{∘}.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $x={30}^{∘}$ and $y={135}^{∘},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-{105}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({30}^{∘}-{135}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{135}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{135}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335835 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51a9cbb7-6557-56b8-b41b-efdef54412d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333737} {:answer/id #uuid "81b1e9cd-c7a9-5810-8b9f-efc4191b4b23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{6}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334141} {:answer/id #uuid "8bbeb5ca-4805-5bb3-a579-86a166eaad07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334236} {:answer/id #uuid "a67c9e7d-c929-590e-863b-c57c5938356f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334445} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa5d35b-d0a6-5ef1-baa6-529dd89b4095" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bbeb5ca-4805-5bb3-a579-86a166eaad07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334236} :answer-field/id #uuid "e62b1cc7-51fe-56a3-bfd4-d5243e6b80e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335640}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4bf1dbaf-6e61-5607-8543-7f1dda28cf3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-65430" :question/problem "What is the exact value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{12}\\right)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $\\frac{11\\pi }{12}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{12}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335836 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "522b93ab-3f47-5712-9880-05e8cf9388af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333744} {:answer/id #uuid "542d47a1-3ca7-59d5-8c9f-1259e146804d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333754} {:answer/id #uuid "67747c50-2a67-54f5-9387-37d37ebb8dca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333934} {:answer/id #uuid "6a805082-626b-5d4a-9a2f-1dffc8566111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333964} {:answer/id #uuid "76e1c814-fee8-5a4c-868b-7bbe395adf32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a805082-626b-5d4a-9a2f-1dffc8566111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333964} :answer-field/id #uuid "55f2a404-a535-5189-b5f0-64b54502ab91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335410}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dddbf82d-3a79-5643-baed-768db6684eef" :question/math-academy-id "q-65437" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335839 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f6c3b03-3d05-5b33-bf74-76ca35cbabaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333271} {:answer/id #uuid "4761f285-4ce8-5ee4-b814-46c24cc30e6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333648} {:answer/id #uuid "5ca6b901-5a7d-5974-a48f-316c1c0fefc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333834} {:answer/id #uuid "eced2d99-93c9-51f1-91b1-74eb9a6cc988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335066} {:answer/id #uuid "f003fbdf-169b-5374-b5dc-383d09ea79f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f003fbdf-169b-5374-b5dc-383d09ea79f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335092} :answer-field/id #uuid "b32e3417-1c93-5649-9e27-6894d1e70346" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335565}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "142983e1-8dc9-5049-86db-6861631dfecc" :question/math-academy-id "q-65457" :question/problem "Simplify ${sin}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x\\cdot {cot}^{2}⁡x+1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the ${sin}^{2}⁡x$ from the first two terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x\\cdot {cot}^{2}⁡x+1 & ={sin}^{2}⁡x(1+{cot}^{2}⁡x)+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity $1+{cot}^{2}⁡x={csc}^{2}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡x(1+{cot}^{2}⁡x)+1 & ={sin}^{2}⁡x({csc}^{2}⁡x)+1 \\\\  & =\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡x}}\\right)+1 \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335841 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cc982e4-c69b-533e-a8fe-cccac358ca96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333248} {:answer/id #uuid "21a31dba-5f24-571d-9520-28aefdb82e0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333289} {:answer/id #uuid "22886b05-c689-5ef5-837b-f8f9a4111abc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333303} {:answer/id #uuid "eea843c9-af8e-5772-a5f2-36de1e49b8c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335083} {:answer/id #uuid "f00173c6-d982-58c8-8d27-bffcb74b9d21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f00173c6-d982-58c8-8d27-bffcb74b9d21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335091} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f2e7169-4d6d-560f-8a19-8b547d6fae2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e53a0ef5-c396-5546-8d82-048f43ee6236" :question/math-academy-id "q-65487" :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to \\pi }{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\pi $ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\pi }{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & ={csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & ={csc}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21e5506f-4577-5036-ada5-dc6f9bc99169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333292} {:answer/id #uuid "366585d6-d0fc-50e1-b496-79591f6c5405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333490} {:answer/id #uuid "75f6de9c-83fb-55b9-9419-ef8f92a5ae22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334056} {:answer/id #uuid "e198d2ca-23db-5649-85de-6116093fd7d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334955} {:answer/id #uuid "ee45bb2e-4cfb-5586-96b6-963f79e9e559" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "366585d6-d0fc-50e1-b496-79591f6c5405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333490} :answer-field/id #uuid "10496ff6-7779-5051-aba6-5bba4e7b9c2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335269}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "baa94592-3dad-5374-a846-3b63aecad625" :question/math-academy-id "q-65571" :question/problem "![](images/2f/2f11d5b11c53f7b826291e0b7e95828f57d6f1242b768baf12c874be229b2935.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{5}+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{5}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{5}(x+\\frac{5C}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{5C}{3}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{5}(x+\\frac{5C}{3}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x}{5}\\right).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/5e/5e9b6568ec493d95db6fe72a6a19024326c5b920629e9ff44a85db54089aa2a6.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x}{5}\\right)$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{3}-0=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5C}{3}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,⟹\\,C=\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{5}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $-\\frac{\\pi }{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335847 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b5b735d-63dc-59e6-b41c-77003c0e40a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333398} {:answer/id #uuid "57318f06-cd50-578b-ae0b-794fe204ec64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333782} {:answer/id #uuid "575d47bf-3cbd-57ee-92d6-60684fb5f9d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333788} {:answer/id #uuid "688f9863-4f33-5417-9de0-c09ccf5fcb8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333949} {:answer/id #uuid "a0da0bdd-42a6-5ec1-a897-867603b5eeb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0da0bdd-42a6-5ec1-a897-867603b5eeb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334401} :answer-field/id #uuid "44d64f22-bb75-503b-b5b4-1ad2bd5bcbcc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e0c19689-c107-5415-8074-e35dadeefb3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-65582" :question/problem "Given that the expression $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{10}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335848 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c99621e-e6a7-5e30-973a-ff97d2ccddc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333099} {:answer/id #uuid "17957d68-04c5-5c1f-9713-201acd7d2a21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333200} {:answer/id #uuid "45e2b4a4-ac60-56b6-babc-d3d8d2937696" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333634} {:answer/id #uuid "b754c4eb-0d5f-585c-b56c-977c36011ad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334596} {:answer/id #uuid "e16defe0-d0e6-5a42-9871-d7729488edd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b754c4eb-0d5f-585c-b56c-977c36011ad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334596} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa2b5f72-65ae-57f9-aa4a-fcc96127719c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "458d6344-5e5f-5019-a823-7a1e4fc1ebe4" :question/math-academy-id "q-65583" :question/problem "Given that the expression $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-ϕ)$ with $0<ϕ<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)=(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=8 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2}{8} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335849 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "139ea022-9338-5e50-93c7-41df72f94394" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333162} {:answer/id #uuid "47b00e5a-492c-5863-805b-d0cb0d3afd0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333652} {:answer/id #uuid "74ca601a-2908-5918-ace8-2c28d3dbd7b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334043} {:answer/id #uuid "d67a4b28-b266-5d2f-84fa-0b31a6f9a9bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334868} {:answer/id #uuid "dc16f2a6-24f9-5edc-98b7-01853a21a64c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47b00e5a-492c-5863-805b-d0cb0d3afd0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333652} :answer-field/id #uuid "35b6bbfb-20e6-55fe-be3e-89d2fd17e1ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "610fb667-dafb-57bd-85ea-670b2220cc0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-65609" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{10}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{10})=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right).\n$$\n\nRewriting the given expression using the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)}} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335861 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b9e80b8-56ad-53a3-b026-16e3818bf6aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333243} {:answer/id #uuid "3346694f-e05f-5c9a-8364-9e84121ce996" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333463} {:answer/id #uuid "398fced3-5d50-539a-85a7-4f195276b87a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333528} {:answer/id #uuid "8203193a-3ef1-55a8-9573-da4a680393e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334147} {:answer/id #uuid "85c2c835-ab1a-5c4f-8204-3e5c80081a50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b9e80b8-56ad-53a3-b026-16e3818bf6aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333243} :answer-field/id #uuid "49903033-3da4-57d6-9ad8-8e12caa8bf6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b5b8276-6c04-5334-a46c-67373ce92195" :question/math-academy-id "q-65851" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the exact solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=1$ for $-2\\pi \\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{(4n+1)\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-2\\pi \\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=-1$ gives $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-2\\pi \\le x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=-\\pi .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335865 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c3fdb35-6cd1-5538-b471-aac5e0cf1d60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\pi +3n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333406} {:answer/id #uuid "32786735-7ca4-590c-bfd3-b217bac2ad84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\pi +4n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333456} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c37a43-f5c2-5bbe-9b01-1249c7985869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\pi +5n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334548} {:answer/id #uuid "ba38102a-de2e-503e-b5f2-18b10b1ba222" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\pi +n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334616} {:answer/id #uuid "e0404905-10a3-5a6e-b256-6a675ff9e249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pi +4n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32786735-7ca4-590c-bfd3-b217bac2ad84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\pi +4n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333456} :answer-field/id #uuid "a95d7e1a-b33e-5efa-81c8-e12bf3a34f9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30e75f0c-aa86-5806-aec9-adbc10da9f1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-65937" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-3\\pi }{2}\\right).$ Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its minimum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer" :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{x-3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the minima of\n\n$$\nf(x)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡X.\n$$\n\nThe $X$-values at the minima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the minima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x-3\\pi }{2} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\pi +2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =2\\pi +4n\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that minimize $f(x)$ are given by $x=2\\pi +4n\\pi ,$  where $n$ is an integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335867 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02ad56ed-db4d-5e79-9a13-c75f23852124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,0]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333011} {:answer/id #uuid "30d17c63-b3df-5151-b92f-0fb3221fcd0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333439} {:answer/id #uuid "7dbe9420-9197-5d3d-a55c-f5ab8d988498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334111} {:answer/id #uuid "80cbbcf7-3954-5302-8248-2549f64847ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334133} {:answer/id #uuid "9ef99f86-dffd-5f20-8a92-9d68798f06d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02ad56ed-db4d-5e79-9a13-c75f23852124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,0]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333011} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d4736da-4c41-5071-8ef0-67391b51ad3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ccf994e-1886-5c8a-88a8-bcf29b5d6ae2" :question/math-academy-id "q-65950" :question/problem "Find the range of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)+1.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- If we add $1$ to the first interval, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)\\le -1 \\\\  & \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)+1\\le -1+1 \\\\  & \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)+1\\le 0 \\\\  & f(x)\\le 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If we add $1$ to the second interval, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)\\ge 1 \\\\  & \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)+1\\ge 1+1 \\\\  & \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)+1\\ge 2 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\le 0,\\,f(x)\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,0]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335873 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2860f091-989b-5acf-bed7-92b08075eb33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333362} {:answer/id #uuid "5040b834-24a3-5df1-a20e-564c56b47beb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333721} {:answer/id #uuid "5f59ee0e-58fc-5a3d-949d-14b9d219ff2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333857} {:answer/id #uuid "8fbc418e-a7e2-54c0-9ca4-dfc253bf86d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334271} {:answer/id #uuid "b17cc870-94e7-5614-a58c-ba7c33e83987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b17cc870-94e7-5614-a58c-ba7c33e83987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334547} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f40443f-a936-5a8c-8318-c86ccbab6f39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335483}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "168eeab0-e1e9-5e07-9123-192a4db7e669" :question/math-academy-id "q-66027" :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $y=-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{sec}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}=6\\pi .$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335876 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "179c1bf3-8a2c-597a-9691-8aa91eb60ce5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-0]\\cup [1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333201} {:answer/id #uuid "8eb9155b-c99f-5edf-bcf7-47ee202d8b80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334262} {:answer/id #uuid "95b7052b-37ce-5396-bb77-088b9296066a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{4}{5}]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334319} {:answer/id #uuid "c9f67f59-e931-5aaa-9a96-f02861af4dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-8]\\cup [8,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334746} {:answer/id #uuid "d20dc049-4ebe-592f-807b-f45ee2cbfd94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-8]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d20dc049-4ebe-592f-807b-f45ee2cbfd94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-8]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334823} :answer-field/id #uuid "91da3a41-f121-5ac4-8e56-5b5d087a0e22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335516}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ebf23ba9-8897-5181-bd95-1d3aa5290158" :question/math-academy-id "q-66062" :question/problem "Find the range of the function $f(x)=5\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)-3.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $5$ and subtracting $3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)\\le -1 \\\\  & 5\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)\\le -5 \\\\  & 5\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)-3\\le -5-3 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second interval by $5$ and subtracting $3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)\\ge 1 \\\\  & 5\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)\\ge 5 \\\\  & 5\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)-3\\ge 5-3 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\le -8,\\,f(x)\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-8]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335890 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09f5104a-6ff3-54f4-aa5c-62ed37ead198" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333080} {:answer/id #uuid "63585c27-7a3a-5092-97b9-8b6f85d39e1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333888} {:answer/id #uuid "73594949-931f-5c38-acef-ed3ae383d30a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334034} {:answer/id #uuid "a44c1fdd-bd1b-547b-a00c-f61e00401b03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334431} {:answer/id #uuid "fac57336-3737-5816-be0e-c9b839b24604" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73594949-931f-5c38-acef-ed3ae383d30a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334034} :answer-field/id #uuid "73908950-ca6a-51b9-b8ec-b5131be094bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd590154-6c93-5483-8fea-428e3503f4be" :question/math-academy-id "q-66603" :question/problem "If $sec⁡x=\\frac{5}{2},$ then what is $4sec⁡(x+10\\pi )-3?$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=sec⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡x=sec⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=5$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡x=sec⁡(x+2\\cdot 5\\cdot \\pi )=sec⁡(x+10\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $sec⁡x=\\frac{5}{2},$ then $sec⁡(x+10\\pi )=\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4sec⁡(x+10\\pi )-3 & =4\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)-3 \\\\  & =2(5)-3 \\\\  & =10-3 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335900 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c07d091-ab22-5255-8a15-bd305458b3ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333245} {:answer/id #uuid "2baad9a6-457a-5a38-a02c-6776c78153a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333401} {:answer/id #uuid "68d7506b-a37c-576e-a779-634ef9970274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333950} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f660e1-8dd3-5fd3-9527-953d86285eec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334735} {:answer/id #uuid "ce918d95-1f0c-5853-9f9b-13e646795f6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8f660e1-8dd3-5fd3-9527-953d86285eec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334735} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb1f7dc8-a87a-5238-af51-cbc6a64e76ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f4ab8e01-c7c3-58b3-9bdf-4dc3acc8551f" :question/math-academy-id "q-66684" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)=3{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2x-1}+7.$ Given that $f(x)\\to a$ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ then $a=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/a3/a3e317bdf1d7c1da85a164331d61dc1500af980ef2b3e907098dd19f42287d8c.png)\n\nSince the function approaches the line $y=7$ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to 7\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=7.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335929 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "350e836a-d347-540c-8c04-df66db7b36da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333476} {:answer/id #uuid "4bb719ab-a22a-56ab-b42c-a89d8cd8e9c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|\\le 1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333685} {:answer/id #uuid "943c7188-2b1c-5397-8a91-d08c229b0c2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334310} {:answer/id #uuid "d67812a4-47f6-5477-ae0d-6c61a7dacb9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Inconclusive because $|r|=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334867} {:answer/id #uuid "e608f853-98f4-5fc1-8300-895e9efcd360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|<1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "943c7188-2b1c-5397-8a91-d08c229b0c2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334310} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b96fa8c-b11c-56f0-bb9a-174f0a700a1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "860416f7-74aa-5f00-b916-8a018898ec49" :question/math-academy-id "q-67458" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series  ${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{5}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{n+1}$ ?" :question/worked-solution "The series starts with $n=3.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{3}{5}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{4+1}}{\\frac{3}{5}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{3+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{5}}{{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335930 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "147d7dd7-5742-52c8-9cea-036c305db51a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333167} {:answer/id #uuid "545ced5d-645e-5b7a-a321-ce6c00a7e910" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333759} {:answer/id #uuid "670c665c-822f-5f27-aeb5-ca19dfcf885b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|<1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333931} {:answer/id #uuid "7e892c02-e1ed-5362-ad52-323e1b1c6d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|<1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334118} {:answer/id #uuid "c1def00b-782b-5957-87a9-4ff969fbb555" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Inconclusive because $|r|=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e892c02-e1ed-5362-ad52-323e1b1c6d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|<1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334118} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b058031-a966-5bca-a29a-785dfd0665ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bb9b4011-1137-576e-a0cc-5ed22e4ce18d" :question/math-academy-id "q-67459" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series \n ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(-4){\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2n}$ ?" :question/worked-solution "The series starts with $n=0.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-4){\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2(1)}}{(-4){\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2(0)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2}}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335931 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "802d1b99-ea38-5d4c-89cd-cb9cf3a96bae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $21$st term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334129} {:answer/id #uuid "aa4f1839-706c-5dea-9ab2-7e5fb5e9a9f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $22$nd term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334482} {:answer/id #uuid "cbadf2ed-0c40-5b91-b1e8-b30929c732f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $24$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334764} {:answer/id #uuid "db911772-c04c-5665-8beb-35d83873bba4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $20$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334909} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d20921-a190-50eb-947b-6de86a1d18e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $23$rd term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db911772-c04c-5665-8beb-35d83873bba4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the $20$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334909} :answer-field/id #uuid "595c0f82-86fe-5794-9976-153201806435" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335417}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f5711a42-9cd4-5351-9262-705bda920d6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-67495" :question/problem "What is the first term that is less than $0.027$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n54,\\,36,\\,24,\\,16,\\,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=54$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{36}{54}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =54\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.027.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.027 \\\\ 54\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{n-1} & <0.027 \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{n-1} & <\\frac{0.027}{54} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{n-1} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,5) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,5) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,5)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,5)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we reversed the inequality sign when divided by $\\operatorname{log}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)$ because $\\operatorname{log}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)$ is a negative number. \n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,5)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}+1≈19.746\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $20$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.027.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335941 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a54a649-fdd6-5ec4-81c1-25723a4e2136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{47.1}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334088} {:answer/id #uuid "7f5f592b-b2ff-5354-ba44-ead6aacd04f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{42.3}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334124} {:answer/id #uuid "9d5d594c-a35e-5f05-ae4a-f5df1b11088f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{31.7}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334376} {:answer/id #uuid "c84d6cb4-2cee-52fa-8de6-0a9379c6bd3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{35.6}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334731} {:answer/id #uuid "ed12f6a1-dfc9-5ed6-835b-c48fbee37dc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45.3}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c84d6cb4-2cee-52fa-8de6-0a9379c6bd3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{35.6}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334731} :answer-field/id #uuid "06db28a8-7c01-5bb4-9c2d-52d54418cb29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aa6fe310-eeef-5a18-a61a-06fedd1010e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-67797" :question/problem "Find the angle between $a=⟨-1,7,0⟩$ and $b=⟨3,6,-1⟩.$ Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." :question/worked-solution "Rearranging the dot product formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 3+7\\cdot 6+0\\cdot (-1)}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{7}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{6}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{39}{\\sqrt{50}\\cdot \\sqrt{46}} \\\\  & =\\frac{39}{\\sqrt{2300}} \\\\  & =\\frac{39}{10\\sqrt{23}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{39}{10\\sqrt{23}}\\right)≈{35.6}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335946 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "142cf96f-53f3-5b6f-bdcf-73a359ab0b6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333166} {:answer/id #uuid "1a6a8233-ba1d-5256-ae17-62beaf3e7d29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333232} {:answer/id #uuid "8a1fe2b5-c224-5f63-bc0e-38f520111296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334221} {:answer/id #uuid "bd46532d-7f38-534f-a68a-6f3926e6afcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334633} {:answer/id #uuid "edd473a6-2783-5ab7-b8c8-6144975fc73d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd46532d-7f38-534f-a68a-6f3926e6afcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334633} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdec7d8b-cde3-5351-955b-03701c3f9af2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335682}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6089db0f-0d34-5714-823b-8d79a6bc501d" :question/math-academy-id "q-68068" :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{4{x}^{3}+6}{5x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}+6}{5x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{x}+\\frac{6}{x})}{\\left(\\frac{5x}{x}\\right)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{x})}{5} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+0)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335948 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74e2bade-d786-5009-8936-cc569536a00e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334044} {:answer/id #uuid "8e041c25-33ad-5a6f-ac1b-1b4d3aa865d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334256} {:answer/id #uuid "966c5f12-12d7-5ef4-8f2f-b9fb3a565c2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334329} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c9fb97-1fbb-55fe-9391-8e38680869c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334410} {:answer/id #uuid "f2bcbec6-0cd5-54cd-9b8d-3df66e0ab3c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74e2bade-d786-5009-8936-cc569536a00e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334044} :answer-field/id #uuid "954cd89b-6679-5a26-9093-39c5477adedc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335521}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1c40af0-3031-581e-91bb-04c8004486f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-68075" :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{5+7{x}^{3}}{12x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5+7{x}^{3}}{12x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{x})}{\\left(\\frac{12x}{x}\\right)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{5}{x}+7{x}^{2})}{12} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(0+7{x}^{2})}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335957 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0489a3d8-49c6-58c6-89d8-9325c6529753" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333025} {:answer/id #uuid "435daf56-24e2-5b7a-a84b-0b267a55f7d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333619} {:answer/id #uuid "aa8e71cb-e233-5326-b597-8faf834611c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334484} {:answer/id #uuid "d4ff1d3f-21eb-5556-aff6-a9d34cc410a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334856} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b9d179-6c2f-5741-8f1b-800322284149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "435daf56-24e2-5b7a-a84b-0b267a55f7d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333619} :answer-field/id #uuid "745c3ef1-1ade-5170-8651-9780fd846441" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26e06283-6379-5d01-aadc-61f2cb42dd71" :question/math-academy-id "q-68213" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{2x}{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{2x}{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\right)=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{2x}{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\right) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {[{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {1}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "125bc6a6-fb74-5cce-8504-caeb0db01da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333153} {:answer/id #uuid "8002ae58-b098-5bda-ad00-0c7449da285b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3x+\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334127} {:answer/id #uuid "88ce2a6a-ee62-5f62-9806-39eacf4d3091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\,\\frac{9}{4}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334202} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd1a297-06d6-51de-b3b8-df8da09b3b58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\,\\frac{12}{5}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334652} {:answer/id #uuid "ea795a17-22c8-5438-a337-67f964713d05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\,\\frac{9}{4}x+\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "125bc6a6-fb74-5cce-8504-caeb0db01da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333153} :answer-field/id #uuid "48542b45-fcfe-5b1a-aede-206d84c3eeaf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dafa44c9-9831-51c8-95fc-4a16466f716d" :question/math-academy-id "q-6858" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-3t,\\,y=2-t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=0$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=0$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={0}^{3}-3\\cdot 0=0-0=0, \\\\ y & =2-0=2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(0,2).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}-3,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-1.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3{t}^{2}-3} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{3{t}^{2}-3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=0,$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=0} & =-\\,\\frac{1}{3\\cdot {0}^{2}-3} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{0-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\,}=-3\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,2)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2 & =-3(x-0) \\\\ y & =-3x+2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335996 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23d44bde-f3ce-5b45-b36c-922997416e90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{3}{0}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333319} {:answer/id #uuid "b0be228a-ade6-5f01-b2fc-7962142adb37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{4}{0}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334541} {:answer/id #uuid "eed15ee9-a2d9-56c9-a612-a673c9741fb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{3}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335084} {:answer/id #uuid "f90856e1-c25b-5776-91b5-b5b1cf938c13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{1}{1}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335172} {:answer/id #uuid "fb20deb5-63e0-503a-93ea-8b4a04e4f152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{4}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb20deb5-63e0-503a-93ea-8b4a04e4f152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{4}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335193} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e5693e5-41e6-5d1c-9d19-1ee996c792dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d33f966c-1019-540f-a11a-1740687ad5e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-69725" :question/problem "Which expression is missing in the following equation?\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{4}{1}\\right)+[]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $5$ and position $2$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe location of $\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)$ in Pascal's triangle is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  &  & \\left(\\frac{0}{0}\\right) \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  &  & \\left(\\frac{1}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{1}{1}\\right) \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & \\left(\\frac{2}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{2}{1}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{2}{2}\\right) \\\\  &  &  &  &  & \\left(\\frac{3}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{3}{1}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{3}{3}\\right) \\\\  &  &  &  & \\left(\\frac{4}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{4}{1}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{4}{2}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{4}{4}\\right) \\\\  &  &  & \\left(\\frac{5}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{5}{1}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{5}{5}\\right) \\\\  &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)$ is the sum of the two closest numbers located in the previous row. So,\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{4}{1}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{4}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\left(\\frac{4}{2}\\right).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335998 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2440e4ad-41fa-518f-9b72-30d0c12b2ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{4}{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333324} {:answer/id #uuid "29604e67-3a3d-596b-bdeb-9445112193fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{3}{1}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333372} {:answer/id #uuid "2a59d6d7-311b-514f-aeb9-ee179f407144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333388} {:answer/id #uuid "57311bcf-e207-5c1c-b7bf-94e48886117f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{4}{1}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333781} {:answer/id #uuid "e419e688-cedf-57db-ac89-e4e3abca16c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2440e4ad-41fa-518f-9b72-30d0c12b2ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{4}{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333324} :answer-field/id #uuid "78e43cdc-a264-5b10-932c-9a10cf458205" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0d912949-b8d4-5987-b2d1-dccb89e2f29e" :question/math-academy-id "q-69727" :question/problem "Which expression is missing in the following equation?\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)+[]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $5$ and position $4$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe location of $\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)$ in Pascal's triangle is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  &  & \\left(\\frac{0}{0}\\right) \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  &  & \\left(\\frac{1}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{1}{1}\\right) \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & \\left(\\frac{2}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{2}{1}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{2}{2}\\right) \\\\  &  &  &  &  & \\left(\\frac{3}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{3}{1}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{3}{3}\\right) \\\\  &  &  &  & \\left(\\frac{4}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{4}{1}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{4}{2}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{4}{4}\\right) \\\\  &  &  & \\left(\\frac{5}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{5}{1}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right) &  & \\left(\\frac{5}{5}\\right) \\\\  &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)$ is the sum of the two closest numbers located in the previous row. So,\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{4}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\left(\\frac{4}{4}\\right).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336001 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f9dea7c-a381-5ac4-81e9-48fc5309db7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333862} {:answer/id #uuid "662c6856-ef40-506d-bd3e-15b7713d4254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333916} {:answer/id #uuid "aaefcdbd-99da-5a68-957d-d4a71e8c97ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334489} {:answer/id #uuid "b9ee0326-0ac0-531f-a543-d7c6c60dd885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334614} {:answer/id #uuid "daa2ab5e-a66b-56f4-b1fa-ffb7112609b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f9dea7c-a381-5ac4-81e9-48fc5309db7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333862} :answer-field/id #uuid "805a371e-0fe9-5fb5-85c5-7fa7d96f5bdb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335486}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "969f8df2-b0c6-59dc-af0d-0ebdb222a078" :question/math-academy-id "q-69752" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{7{x}^{2}-27x-70}{(2x+3)(4-x)(x+2)}=\\frac{A}{(2x+3)}+\\frac{B}{(4-x)}+\\frac{C}{(x+2)},\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $B$ and $C$ are real constants, calculate $A+B+C.$" :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A,$ $B$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7{x}^{2}-27x-70}{(2x+3)(4-x)(x+2)}=\\frac{A}{(2x+3)}+\\frac{B}{(4-x)}+\\frac{C}{(x+2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(2x+3)(4-x)(x+2)$ gives\n\n$$\n7{x}^{2}-27x-70=A(4-x)(x+2)+B(2x+3)(x+2)+C(2x+3)(4-x).\n$$\n\nFor $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}-27\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)-70 & =A(4-\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right))(\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)+2) \\\\  & \\,+B(2\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)+3)(\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)+2) \\\\  & \\,+C(2\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)+3)(4-\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)) \\\\ \\frac{63}{4}+\\frac{81}{2}-70 & =\\frac{11}{4}A \\\\ -\\frac{55}{4} & =\\frac{11}{4}A \\\\ A & =-\\frac{55}{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{11} \\\\ A & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=4$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(4)^{2}-27(4)-70 & =A(4-4)(4+2) \\\\  & \\,+B(2(4)+3)(4+2) \\\\  & \\,+C(2(4)+3)(4-4) \\\\ 112-108-70 & =B(11)(6) \\\\ B & =\\frac{-66}{66} \\\\ B & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-2$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(-2)^{2}-27(-2)-70 & =A(4-(-2))((-2)+2) \\\\  & \\,+B(2(-2)+3)((-2)+2) \\\\  & \\,+C(2(-2)+3)(4-(-2)) \\\\ 28+54-70 & =C(-1)(6) \\\\ C & =\\frac{12}{-6} \\\\ C & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B+C=-5+(-1)+(-2)=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336006 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3eb6ce64-206d-5c49-9758-aab85d9dbcfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{(4x-1)}-\\frac{4}{(x+3)}+\\frac{3}{(x+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333581} {:answer/id #uuid "4cc83462-f743-5a31-a878-586134f09bdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{(4x-1)}-\\frac{1}{(x+3)}-\\frac{2}{(x+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333696} {:answer/id #uuid "8e45fd15-a700-53e7-af9d-1e68321c0c82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(4x-1)}-\\frac{6}{(x+3)}+\\frac{3}{(x+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334260} {:answer/id #uuid "b1405896-e848-5a48-a42b-fcc5e198319b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{(4x-1)}-\\frac{4}{(x+3)}-\\frac{2}{(x+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334545} {:answer/id #uuid "db4473c1-24e2-509c-87f1-abaa53777db0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{(4x-1)}+\\frac{6}{(x+3)}+\\frac{1}{(x+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3eb6ce64-206d-5c49-9758-aab85d9dbcfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{(4x-1)}-\\frac{4}{(x+3)}+\\frac{3}{(x+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333581} :answer-field/id #uuid "20d9cbeb-76f4-5a13-95f0-cb183cadc1c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fdf4b6e8-9579-5ff0-a940-fe50fbaf31e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-69820" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{-2{x}^{2}+29x+1}{(4x-1)(x+3)(x+1)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A,$ $B$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-2{x}^{2}+29x+1}{(4x-1)(x+3)(x+1)} & =\\frac{A}{(4x-1)}+\\frac{B}{(x+3)}+\\frac{C}{(x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(4x-1)(x+3)(x+1)$ gives\n\n$$\n-2{x}^{2}+29x+1=A(x+3)(x+1)+B(4x-1)(x+1)+C(4x-1)(x+3).\n$$\n\nFor $x=\\frac{1}{4}$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}+29\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)+1 & =A(\\frac{1}{4}+3)(\\frac{1}{4}+1) \\\\  & \\,+B(4\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-1)(\\frac{1}{4}+1) \\\\  & \\,+C(4\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-1)(\\frac{1}{4}+3) \\\\ -\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{29}{4}+1 & =A\\left(\\frac{65}{16}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{65}{8} & =A\\left(\\frac{65}{16}\\right) \\\\ A & =\\frac{16}{8} \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-3$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(-3)^{2}+29(-3)+1 & =A(-3+3)(-3+1) \\\\  & \\,+B(4(-3)-1)(-3+1) \\\\  & \\,+C(4(-3)-1)(-3+3) \\\\ -18-87+1 & =B(-13)(-2) \\\\ B & =\\frac{-104}{26} \\\\ B & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-1$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(-1)^{2}+29(-1)+1 & =A(-1+3)(-1+1) \\\\  & \\,+B(4(-1)-1)(-1+1) \\\\  & \\,+C(4(-1)-1)(-1+3) \\\\ -2-29+1 & =C(-5)(2) \\\\ C & =\\frac{-30}{-10} \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{-2{x}^{2}+29x+1}{(4x-1)(x+3)(x+1)}=\\frac{2}{(4x-1)}-\\frac{4}{(x+3)}+\\frac{3}{(x+1)}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336009 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23e9867c-2070-5bab-b192-73c114d856e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{27}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333320} {:answer/id #uuid "5702b26d-b0dc-5e0f-ba2a-878b7edfd671" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{63}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333779} {:answer/id #uuid "946a6bdc-ef7c-5906-9ecd-f3fe81a3df39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334312} {:answer/id #uuid "af6d14b0-24b6-5bc9-b225-b55c80866b32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{57}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334535} {:answer/id #uuid "f4dd0804-168a-5b02-a849-c7aaa5586513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{88}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5702b26d-b0dc-5e0f-ba2a-878b7edfd671" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{63}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333779} :answer-field/id #uuid "092f11eb-2564-5c78-8d82-f69b65e5aeec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "738d368b-e30a-5573-8091-e990b5e1acb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-69831" :question/problem "Find $∠QPR$ given the points $P(2,1,2)$, $Q(1,3,2)$, and $R(4,5,-2).$ Round your answer to the nearest degree." :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $P$, $Q$, $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨2,1,2⟩,\\,q=⟨1,3,2⟩,\\,r=⟨4,5,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠QPR=\\theta .$\n\n![](images/30/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨1,3,2⟩-⟨2,1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =⟨4,5,-2⟩-⟨2,1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,4,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 4+0\\cdot (-4)}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{6\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\right)≈{63}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336010 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "321940d9-bd6b-529f-b36b-52de6034ea1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{120}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333452} {:answer/id #uuid "47c034fd-ff2c-563e-807c-0bd209a7f45d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333653} {:answer/id #uuid "5d29e042-7c4a-5adf-9528-5bd45b78ba7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333842} {:answer/id #uuid "7fc9f4bc-7a23-5d72-9079-6503dce5faa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334126} {:answer/id #uuid "c99908af-2c65-5635-a798-8ecd8753f621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fc9f4bc-7a23-5d72-9079-6503dce5faa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334126} :answer-field/id #uuid "e443c3bb-f95f-5b04-a2c2-a7d665f716b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335635}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1615350f-44ad-5e4b-849c-6c410f4c58d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-69833" :question/problem "Find $∠XZY$ given the points $X(-1,0,3)$, $Y(-2,2,3)$, and $Z(-2,-1,3).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $X$, $Y$, $Z$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=⟨-1,0,3⟩,\\,y=⟨-2,2,3⟩,\\,z=⟨-2,-1,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $△XYZ$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠XZY=\\theta .$ \n\n![](images/87/87453fecb9da6b9e673ab5a85a289a3fac73f4fb90fc8f1b33e7c0a80b1ea77f.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{ZX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{ZY}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{ZX} & =x-z \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,3⟩-⟨-2,-1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{ZY} & =y-z \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,3⟩-⟨-2,-1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,3,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{ZX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{ZY}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{ZX}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{ZY}}{|\\overset{\\to }{ZX}||\\overset{\\to }{ZY}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 3+0\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)={45}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336025 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1949a4de-c812-5072-95ba-5ca1acc38a34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333220} {:answer/id #uuid "5072c3c1-6a38-59b1-ab30-b0e796a30b24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333722} {:answer/id #uuid "7c20c4e2-d46e-53a3-a715-970b210bf453" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334098} {:answer/id #uuid "d3988da9-7004-5644-aa59-103a31972103" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334844} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ad36fa-50d8-5246-9fe7-83f484f60cda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335114}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2ad36fa-50d8-5246-9fe7-83f484f60cda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335114} :answer-field/id #uuid "57d6563e-a550-56cd-86eb-ddc3caa46b33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "16ddeea5-cbc1-599e-ac3c-ae0bcf472762" :question/math-academy-id "q-69946" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\text{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 4 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 4 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =1-1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,0\\,|=0\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336027 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2aeea89d-c2e4-5d69-8124-57f15b02cda8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2.214)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2.214))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333395} {:answer/id #uuid "3b3f10a4-8058-543d-927f-ba55fc58275f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4.069)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4.069))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333547} {:answer/id #uuid "b49c7d6a-0555-5f84-ba9d-67606386145a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3.927)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3.927))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334571} {:answer/id #uuid "c4843f11-0725-5908-be86-78378b5430a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4.069)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4.069))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334692} {:answer/id #uuid "fa9c2df5-6ed0-572b-bb36-0c624b6010ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2.214)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2.214))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b3f10a4-8058-543d-927f-ba55fc58275f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4.069)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4.069))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333547} :answer-field/id #uuid "e90bff07-3ac0-5b51-b33a-714cc5eeebbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "64737006-09f0-5f7b-b3c3-c7aaff447f72" :question/math-academy-id "q-69962" :question/problem "Express $z=\\frac{10}{-3+4\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give $\\theta $ in radians to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $-3-4\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{10}{-3+4\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10(-3-4\\text{i})}{((-3)+4\\text{i})((-3)-4\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{10(-3-4\\text{i})}{(-3)^{2}-16{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-30-40\\text{i}}{9+16} \\\\  & =-\\frac{30}{25}-\\frac{40}{25}\\text{i} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{5}-\\frac{8}{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=-\\frac{6}{5}-\\frac{8}{5}\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{6}{5}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{8}{5}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{6}^{2}+{8}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{100}{25}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=-\\frac{6}{5}$ and $y=-\\frac{8}{5}$ are both negative, $z$ lies in the third quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{8}{5}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{6}{5}\\right)}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right) \\\\  & ≈4.069,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. \n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4.069)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4.069))\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336028 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b94c9b6-5a65-514c-8c57-8a32c5352b1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5.381)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5.381))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333682} {:answer/id #uuid "61306afb-39b1-55fe-940d-c895a219866a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5.176)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5.176))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333873} {:answer/id #uuid "96c4c078-1d80-57b1-a631-8e1db6092f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4.381)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4.381))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334332} {:answer/id #uuid "c496a847-8027-5fcd-afe4-d23fd213b900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3.107)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3.107))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334693} {:answer/id #uuid "cd5f1dfc-0f11-5087-ab8d-bd8e85c01e76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4.914)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4.914))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61306afb-39b1-55fe-940d-c895a219866a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5.176)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5.176))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333873} :answer-field/id #uuid "9159f624-aea2-5058-8642-6d5fa9463a5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2ba9feb4-93ae-58b8-887d-a3260d202f9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-69966" :question/problem "Express $z=\\frac{4}{1+2\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give $\\theta $ in radians to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $1-2\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{4}{1+2\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1-2\\text{i})}{(1+2\\text{i})(1-2\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1-2\\text{i})}{{1}^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1-2\\text{i})}{1+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{8}{5}\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{8}{5}\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{8}{5}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{4}^{2}+{8}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{80}{25}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{16\\cdot 5}{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=\\frac{4}{5}$ is positive and $y=-\\frac{8}{5}$ is negative, $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{8}{5}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡2 \\\\  & ≈5.176,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. \n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5.176)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5.176)).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338728 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7add7aa6-bbf7-5029-8640-2e8f14c436db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-1+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337141} {:answer/id #uuid "b131419d-27a7-5aed-a8b3-097458d0255d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=1-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337620} {:answer/id #uuid "b5eac5c2-f481-5fa9-8048-ece28c0bc150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337651} {:answer/id #uuid "f5fab424-bb15-5d36-98d3-c9350025487a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{3}-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338184} {:answer/id #uuid "f63ac1a9-b22d-5980-9ce4-d9ff57a87d52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=1-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b131419d-27a7-5aed-a8b3-097458d0255d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=1-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337620} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a219c54-5dfc-5f18-bef5-25d3ec1a3a25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6b3421d1-d66d-525b-ac62-73352da1e4fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-69967" :question/problem "Express $z=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right))$ in the form $z=x+\\text{i}y.$" :question/worked-solution "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nFirst, we find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right):$\n \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\n \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the values and expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{2}-\\text{i}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338737 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "458abcc9-f9d2-5ac1-90af-c3e51ddbeaf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336678} {:answer/id #uuid "495b8260-a49e-5a8f-9920-aa1452cc6b14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336714} {:answer/id #uuid "b58864db-70c6-53b7-a529-c59a7eb41b7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337649} {:answer/id #uuid "c056af8b-a1b0-5a78-922a-5959d7114e84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337740} {:answer/id #uuid "f661253a-01bb-54b0-a96e-abb72c2d9cf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "495b8260-a49e-5a8f-9920-aa1452cc6b14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336714} :answer-field/id #uuid "38312ade-1b62-5072-b574-59237a3234e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c3915b78-e613-51a4-bee3-c8d5f4801fc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-69993" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1+3x}{(x-1)(x+1)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{x-1}+\\frac{B}{x+1}+\\frac{C}{(x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{D}{(x+1)^{3}},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$, $B$, $C$ and $D$ are real constants, find the value of $AB-CD.$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x-1)(x+1)^{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n1+3x=A(x+1)^{3}+B(x-1)(x+1)^{2}+C(x-1)(x+1)+D(x-1).\n$$\n\nLetting $x=-1$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+3(-1) & =A(-1+1)^{3}+B(-1-1)(-1+1)^{2}+C(-1-1)(-1+1)+D(-1-1) \\\\ 1-3 & =-2D \\\\ -2 & =-2D \\\\ D & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+3(1) & =A(1+1)^{3}+B(1-1)(1+1)^{2}+C(1-1)(1+1)+D(1-1) \\\\ 1+3 & =8A \\\\ 4 & =8A \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$ and using our values for $A$ and $D$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+3(0) & =\\frac{1}{2}(0+1)^{3}+B(0-1)(0+1)^{2}+C(0-1)(0+1)+1(0-1) \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{1}{2}-B-C-1 \\\\ C & =-\\frac{3}{2}-B.\\,\\,(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, letting $x=2$ and using our values for $A$ and $D$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+3(2) & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+1)^{3}+B(2-1)(2+1)^{2}+C(2-1)(2+1)+1(2-1) \\\\ 1+6 & =\\frac{27}{2}+9B+3C+1 \\\\ 3C & =-\\frac{15}{2}-9B \\\\ C & =-\\frac{5}{2}-3B.\\,\\,(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom $(1)$ and $(2)$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}-B & =-\\frac{5}{2}-3B \\\\ 2B & =-1 \\\\ B & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the value of $B$ into $(1)$  to get the value of $C:$\n\n$$\nC=-\\frac{3}{2}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-1\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nAB-CD=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-(-1)(1)=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338777 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50438df5-858e-5d3e-b468-8c7b676fadb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336767} {:answer/id #uuid "80a82059-5fb8-5c9c-a5a8-63bfac931a04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337198} {:answer/id #uuid "9356b697-bff7-5a9c-ad2c-238415671b77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337375} {:answer/id #uuid "c147368a-c6c1-5584-a143-67ac5c21e1c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337749} {:answer/id #uuid "ea6c60e3-0e49-59c1-a054-afd45888a652" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50438df5-858e-5d3e-b468-8c7b676fadb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336767} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a93c500-a992-5f23-ba79-5045bff776b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "efe0a2b9-714b-5ee9-b856-1df07a8695d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-70311" :question/problem "Determine the value of $\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "The binomial coefficient $\\left(\\frac{n}{r}\\right)$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{n}{r}\\right)=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $n=7$ and $r=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right) & =\\frac{7!}{(7-3)!\\cdot 3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot 6\\cdot 5\\cdot 4!}{4!\\cdot 3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot \\cancel{6}\\cdot 5\\cdot \\cancel{4!}}{\\cancel{4!}\\cdot \\cancel{6}} \\\\  & =7\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =35.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338805 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ac5b001-3222-5f20-9cfa-a6a178edbcf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1+\\sqrt{t}}{\\sqrt{t}},\\,\\frac{1}{t},\\,16{t}^{4}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336134} {:answer/id #uuid "43211d42-ef9b-5294-9784-f61afc4354fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2\\sqrt{t},\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{t},\\,2{t}^{4}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336665} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0b1790-c755-5de3-8e68-28d3776dc12c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4\\sqrt{t},\\,\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,8t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336737} {:answer/id #uuid "adc3c998-c9bf-5aa6-bbfb-3bb4b5b897c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{t}},\\,\\frac{t-4}{{t}^{2}},\\,4{t}^{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337591} {:answer/id #uuid "d10a81b8-09d6-54a4-86e1-fa3b6c978c0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1+8\\sqrt{t}}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,\\frac{t-4}{{t}^{2}},\\,16{t}^{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337863}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d10a81b8-09d6-54a4-86e1-fa3b6c978c0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1+8\\sqrt{t}}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,\\frac{t-4}{{t}^{2}},\\,16{t}^{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337863} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a6a4498-4274-5a8f-a340-b4c6887cef4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bba82f4d-eee0-52ff-96d4-e8a4604b03f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-70681" :question/problem "Differentiate $f(t)+4g(t)$ with respect to $t$, given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨\\sqrt{t},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,2⟩ \\\\ g(t) & =⟨t,\\,\\frac{1}{t},\\,{t}^{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple rule and the sum rule, we have\n\n$$\n(f(t)+4g(t))^{′}={f}^{′}(t)+4{g}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,\\frac{1}{t},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,4{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f(t)+4g(t))^{′} & ={f}^{′}(t)+4{g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,\\frac{1}{t},\\,0⟩+4⟨1,\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,4{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,\\frac{1}{t},\\,0⟩+⟨4,\\,-\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}},\\,16{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+4,\\,\\frac{1}{t}-\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}},\\,0+16{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1+8\\sqrt{t}}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,\\frac{t-4}{{t}^{2}},\\,16{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338841 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20324c7d-2431-5dbf-89ba-4a62fbbbbdeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\frac{3}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336333} {:answer/id #uuid "2d601842-f51f-5a34-901c-3c87ed74673e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\pi }-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336467} {:answer/id #uuid "c6ecc1e0-b453-5588-a3e8-923b6e7ebcab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337797} {:answer/id #uuid "ec9d4934-d293-54d1-9eb4-aaf1a8bf5781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338096} {:answer/id #uuid "ef4a666c-1131-5d8c-a6c2-62062090c6e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\pi }+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d601842-f51f-5a34-901c-3c87ed74673e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\pi }-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336467} :answer-field/id #uuid "743b193f-17c2-5199-b0eb-adc69b1674c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b4b4054a-401d-511c-ba0c-65722b1ecab6" :question/math-academy-id "q-70874" :question/problem "Find the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ if $f(x)=ln⁡x+3csc⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative using the sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡x+3csc⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}+3(-csc⁡xcot⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}-3csc⁡xcot⁡x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ in the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)}-3csc⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)cot⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\pi }-3\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\pi }-3\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\pi }-2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338891 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53623a55-b77d-5351-9fc2-d973dfa306f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(-1)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336802} {:answer/id #uuid "79c71031-97e9-5e1d-a6ba-e74f9e41cc73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337129} {:answer/id #uuid "976f1ad0-705e-5259-a60b-4f7ca8ac9ef3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337413} {:answer/id #uuid "aaa26b4e-cfda-5c6f-8638-0760a72b3b60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337570} {:answer/id #uuid "fb13ed6b-6c19-5ee0-9d99-95a01f90eaeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79c71031-97e9-5e1d-a6ba-e74f9e41cc73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337129} :answer-field/id #uuid "af56593a-6a9e-589a-b62d-3c3c78f2abea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338599}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c90a0801-6b26-5a6a-b72a-693261024597" :question/math-academy-id "q-71079" :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ given below have a discontinuity at $x=-1?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2}, & x<-1 \\\\ 2, & x=-1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}, & x>-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1.$\n\n- $f(-1)=2,$ so $f(-1)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}+1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1}{2} \\\\  & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-1)^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=1$ $✓$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=1,$ while $f(-1)=2.$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-1$ because ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338906 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29f861a8-2302-5786-875b-0483712b1672" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{z}^{3}}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336420} {:answer/id #uuid "3743933c-7638-5290-ac53-5c374b98a41f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{z}^{4}}{8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336545} {:answer/id #uuid "39b7b862-80f6-54ad-a2dd-adf18742228f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{z}^{4}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336575} {:answer/id #uuid "6d5d628d-9b82-52f5-8b6d-3a0ca034b61c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{z}^{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337002} {:answer/id #uuid "865dbfb1-63e9-595b-8bd8-7ea55e38308a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{z}^{4}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3743933c-7638-5290-ac53-5c374b98a41f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{z}^{4}}{8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336545} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bccbede-e1db-5241-8aaa-dd88aca0dae9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338410}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "910c5e68-2775-5527-90b5-7207dda21902" :question/math-academy-id "q-71162" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{{z}^{3}}{2}\\,\\text{d}z.$" :question/worked-solution "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{z}^{3}}{2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int {z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{{z}^{3+1}}{3+1}\\right)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{z}^{4}}{4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{z}^{4}}{8}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338907 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09b6ff67-4138-58ea-94dd-6bb8c9ebd100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336118} {:answer/id #uuid "828a543e-18e7-558b-9b8a-5619826dd00a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{5}}{12}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337219} {:answer/id #uuid "b563b75d-32af-5225-9bcc-e72ef5253840" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{6}}{12}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337648} {:answer/id #uuid "ee0ea1f9-08f7-5950-8c1a-c412188e036f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338113} {:answer/id #uuid "f5d3daa8-503d-543b-91dd-745b2a60d6b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{x}^{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee0ea1f9-08f7-5950-8c1a-c412188e036f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338113} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4f2b542-6f34-56ff-ab94-00255931de9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "83815382-7a50-585c-99ac-149a3085c008" :question/math-academy-id "q-71164" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int {x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{5+1}}{5+1}\\right)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6}}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338908 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d8d8472-3abf-5397-bb1e-f42c4b1ae7cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336307} {:answer/id #uuid "37ed7c0a-79f6-5272-bd35-2662e9c03587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{x}^{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336551} {:answer/id #uuid "543a3a68-ee3b-56cc-9d55-b7fe4b24b74a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{x}^{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336810} {:answer/id #uuid "895d314a-f1f0-5387-b5b7-a9e0d9a5a268" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{x}^{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337270} {:answer/id #uuid "d0002111-f915-5c84-8ac7-a2e9f9ed6fe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{x}^{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "543a3a68-ee3b-56cc-9d55-b7fe4b24b74a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{x}^{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336810} :answer-field/id #uuid "092a56d4-2594-5382-a5b9-d778520c9056" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f9197c9-6545-5b05-9e0e-dc89f6ab104f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71165" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int (-8{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-8{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =-8\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-8\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3+1}}{3+1}\\right)+C \\\\  & =-8\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C \\\\  & =-2{x}^{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338929 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "157bc3da-2d1c-5137-b516-d8ff7c42bad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336235} {:answer/id #uuid "3cfe7ebf-3262-597f-b2fd-edf534fd618c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336607} {:answer/id #uuid "4baa559e-cde4-5072-9145-09ac20cb641a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336735} {:answer/id #uuid "829583d7-8e78-579d-9221-64ce8b48dc24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337220} {:answer/id #uuid "e26af925-4997-53e5-8b3d-7425b92d8e93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4baa559e-cde4-5072-9145-09ac20cb641a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336735} :answer-field/id #uuid "e48649d2-bf76-55fc-aef9-b0aff8e82c8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "22cfc584-e8e6-55e0-a1d3-35d71c5742a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-71370" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$, can be found by computing $∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}) & =2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\text{ }i+2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})}{‖∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})‖} & =\\frac{-i+j}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-i+j}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338930 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "049b7f23-d6a7-5be3-a6ca-ac19b8a84234" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336070} {:answer/id #uuid "123c36c6-7310-581c-8de4-b675e090dc56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336203} {:answer/id #uuid "4b49d759-d015-5843-b6e9-3497dc09edfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336730} {:answer/id #uuid "6e16ffd6-406d-50b5-9b6a-0f4b899fc6bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337009} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd3f738-ef19-5d09-afcb-04a5b6bdd7fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(-2i+i)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e16ffd6-406d-50b5-9b6a-0f4b899fc6bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337009} :answer-field/id #uuid "60184a90-8959-560b-aa4a-378830d13dbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "436e814d-020d-5b28-b909-495bbb1c431a" :question/math-academy-id "q-71374" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=x+3y-2xy$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(\\frac{3}{2},1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(\\frac{3}{2},1)$, can be found by computing $∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(\\frac{3}{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+3y-2xy)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+3y-2xy)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(1-2y)\\text{ }i+(3-2x)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1) & =(1-2(1))\\text{ }i+(3-2\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1)}{‖∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1)‖} & =\\frac{-i}{1}=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338958 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "095f0794-39e0-5248-a897-200aa365fb49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336115} {:answer/id #uuid "3b3334b6-e096-5561-85b8-d0e63cefc06f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336589} {:answer/id #uuid "67a09e4b-a7d7-5075-9742-255d4d1d82c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336957} {:answer/id #uuid "b9bb0e9e-f8a4-5eca-8ee8-abae6195f1c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337678} {:answer/id #uuid "c5c71b4a-acd4-54cc-98ec-eb1e46b34922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b3334b6-e096-5561-85b8-d0e63cefc06f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336589} :answer-field/id #uuid "90dbd918-3070-505f-8d67-8a3bd6620730" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34e4c933-c949-5c6b-b0d7-3414a5ba0bf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-71527" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,p\\,|=\\sqrt{3}$ and the angle between vectors $p$ and $q$ is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Find the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p & =|\\,p\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338959 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21ef2e36-7c06-57fe-a2e8-27848b286f5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336349} {:answer/id #uuid "32bc2709-4755-5539-a2ad-575115b181b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336512} {:answer/id #uuid "46863dc5-85a6-5f74-b07a-395c884b21c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336686} {:answer/id #uuid "cc073af2-ce94-51b6-9620-4721296451cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337822} {:answer/id #uuid "e0bd5231-b4ba-55f8-840d-4883556a842d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0bd5231-b4ba-55f8-840d-4883556a842d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337994} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbc5ca9d-1270-51e9-8a5b-a7cb066ec78d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338664}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c0a3ab5c-4820-575a-9c45-dc5563c40e29" :question/math-academy-id "q-71530" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,p\\,|=3\\sqrt{2}$ and the angle between vectors $p$ and $q$ is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Find the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p & =|\\,p\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2af732f3-b0dc-5793-a81b-2631dc11f520" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\text{ arcsec }x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336435} {:answer/id #uuid "733c5a21-c647-52ab-a919-8a80bbbead81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\text{ arcsin }x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337069} {:answer/id #uuid "75c8a2d2-a396-596b-8050-fe63de4a5851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\text{ arctan }x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337091} {:answer/id #uuid "95948138-a8a6-5445-b9c9-fdd287efee17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\text{ arctan }x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337398} {:answer/id #uuid "e59b54b8-9fef-5db5-8ace-2e476c52cf06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\text{ arcsec }x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338036}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e59b54b8-9fef-5db5-8ace-2e476c52cf06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\text{ arcsec }x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338036} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f257272-2f12-50ae-a6fd-7184a295f711" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c37f5da7-d3e9-5087-b956-7bcf82e01ec3" :question/math-academy-id "q-71775" :question/problem "If ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{9}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}},$ then $f(x)=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{arcsec}\\,x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{9}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{9}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9\\text{ arcsec }x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339000 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0b97a6b-e1ff-50f5-ab1f-37b3ab282c83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337742} {:answer/id #uuid "c2e7480f-dbd5-5885-8503-7a72787be86f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337763} {:answer/id #uuid "d0bdf6fc-3892-5112-a046-b04e54d7df85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337858} {:answer/id #uuid "d0d3c6c7-ecd4-50be-8fef-f7432eaa4b10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337860} {:answer/id #uuid "d629248a-72f1-5ef2-a0a0-24852eab0c8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d629248a-72f1-5ef2-a0a0-24852eab0c8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337897} :answer-field/id #uuid "d281a134-374b-5aef-8d0d-6fbd0b5e1009" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47b269c9-2c28-5ab1-9f15-6a708a836ca9" :question/math-academy-id "q-71835" :question/problem "Suppose that $|\\,p\\,|=8$, $|\\,q\\,|=4$ and that the angle between the vectors is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Find the vector projection of $p$ onto $q$ in terms of the vector $q.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the scalar projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p & =|\\,p\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the unit vector that is parallel to $q:$\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{q}{|\\,q\\,|}=\\frac{q}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}q\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the vector projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,p & ={\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p\\cdot u \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}q \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}q\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339031 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18b1a026-0c8e-5be6-adb1-b801d9462350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\,{\\text{e}}^{\\pi \\text{i}/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336261} {:answer/id #uuid "6b815872-9150-5fbc-857f-7f8878fc0406" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{e}}^{\\pi \\text{i}/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336982} {:answer/id #uuid "ae2f5c7d-8fc0-5b07-9d5b-195b41b0ea1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{e}}^{\\pi \\text{i}/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337595} {:answer/id #uuid "ae59604e-758d-5ce6-92bf-5957adffda25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{e}}^{\\pi \\text{i}/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337597} {:answer/id #uuid "e45cdb94-229c-56a8-9e36-06a47e7f880e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{e}}^{\\pi \\text{i}/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e45cdb94-229c-56a8-9e36-06a47e7f880e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{e}}^{\\pi \\text{i}/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338022} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cc68346-3208-5592-a11e-7e16cd3cadd8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "44f829a5-c105-55cc-8692-b54dc70c107f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71972" :question/problem "![](images/a0/a0d3f6646cb3a1faa99ba07e76956faf9be1c6f117191c539aae63e3fc1a2ee1.png)\n\nWrite the complex number $z$, shown on the Argand diagram above, in exponential form." :question/worked-solution "The complex number shown in the Argand diagram can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x+\\text{i}y \\\\  & =2+2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo write it in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the first quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=2\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get \n\n$$\nz=2\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{e}}^{\\pi \\text{i}/4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339033 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68df4ce4-7b4c-5e9c-8971-c2ebb0327320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\,{\\text{e}}^{4\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336966} {:answer/id #uuid "81c90edc-c237-58b1-99e4-32f3439329d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\,{\\text{e}}^{7\\pi \\text{i}/6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337212} {:answer/id #uuid "954ed73e-542a-5e76-a0d5-ab5936848fc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=3\\,{\\text{e}}^{7\\pi \\text{i}/6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337396} {:answer/id #uuid "d3200542-63c9-542a-b4fa-3a305e3f0daa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=3\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{e}}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337877} {:answer/id #uuid "f89bb0ec-5438-5500-941a-c85a89ff1e15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=3\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{e}}^{4\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338210}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81c90edc-c237-58b1-99e4-32f3439329d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\,{\\text{e}}^{7\\pi \\text{i}/6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337212} :answer-field/id #uuid "f557b58a-3824-50b5-98d1-b94b8a347248" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "44bd86e8-5f62-5bc0-b074-fc3bcd405c3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-71977" :question/problem "![](images/a0/a02f84856db56412060d6f376f3d926e3fb05581ee36ef9b5e4d2cc570e8438d.png)\n\nWrite the complex number $z$, shown on the Argand diagram above, in exponential form." :question/worked-solution "The complex number shown in the Argand diagram can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x+\\text{i}y \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}-\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo write it in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the third quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)| \\\\  & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{-1}{-\\sqrt{3}}\\right)| \\\\  & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=2$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get \n\n$$\nz=2\\,{\\text{e}}^{7\\pi \\text{i}/6}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339034 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "104e3006-d3a6-5943-b960-4fc26ccc0775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4\\sqrt{3}\\,{\\text{e}}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336185} {:answer/id #uuid "48e092bf-cba4-5a3c-8a77-c83349f80761" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4\\sqrt{3}\\,{\\text{e}}^{11\\pi \\text{i}/6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336709} {:answer/id #uuid "925512aa-b662-5d52-a43e-43e72f39de57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\sqrt{3}\\,{\\text{e}}^{7\\pi \\text{i}/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337361} {:answer/id #uuid "bcae6fb2-56b5-5b2d-9294-f949c5b05c85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\sqrt{3}\\,{\\text{e}}^{11\\pi \\text{i}/6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337711} {:answer/id #uuid "fbeb787b-6f45-55c2-9d88-54fe66eb199b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\sqrt{3}\\,{\\text{e}}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcae6fb2-56b5-5b2d-9294-f949c5b05c85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\sqrt{3}\\,{\\text{e}}^{11\\pi \\text{i}/6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337711} :answer-field/id #uuid "6008bb85-5b57-5579-9250-2b4fcfa665b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8bd2c718-1e1f-5805-84c5-97c49ff95cca" :question/math-academy-id "q-71979" :question/problem "Write the complex number $z=3-\\text{i}\\sqrt{3}$ in exponential form." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{(-\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=2\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nz=2\\sqrt{3}\\,{\\text{e}}^{11\\pi \\text{i}/6}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339035 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07fbe631-b6ca-5a02-9887-621761e1d977" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=3\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{e}}^{7\\pi \\text{i}/6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336098} {:answer/id #uuid "21429edd-1977-51a1-bcfd-f43edb6488b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\,{\\text{e}}^{7\\pi \\text{i}/6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336341} {:answer/id #uuid "6702c7dc-5c67-5f1c-800b-a01eb18a4c95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=3\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{e}}^{4\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336951} {:answer/id #uuid "9d76044f-ef19-533b-b862-ccd1b90bfdca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\,{\\text{e}}^{4\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337456} {:answer/id #uuid "f5353090-574e-5ac6-a153-11a0a199d745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\,{\\text{e}}^{3\\pi \\text{i}/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21429edd-1977-51a1-bcfd-f43edb6488b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\,{\\text{e}}^{7\\pi \\text{i}/6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336341} :answer-field/id #uui