swer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4\\,{\\text{e}}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337086} {:answer/id #uuid "a7fb877f-dfa1-5ad1-a96d-bc31e563bd26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4\\,{\\text{e}}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337551} {:answer/id #uuid "ce965180-c460-5735-ba71-a16c78ddb8a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\,{\\text{e}}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74eb7548-5b21-5ab1-b315-9aba5080f6e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4\\,{\\text{e}}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337086} :answer-field/id #uuid "2137d245-5e38-588c-bf3d-27b3cfc9ed11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a3bc38ff-6706-540c-bfc8-3a2f9e0a0e13" :question/math-academy-id "q-71982" :question/problem "Write the complex number $z=-2+2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}$ in exponential form." :question/worked-solution "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{(2\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the second quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)| \\\\  & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)| \\\\  & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=4$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get \n\n$$\nz=4\\,{\\text{e}}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339084 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3906178d-d2b9-5c1e-8874-62464ff4587d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336568} {:answer/id #uuid "485ed612-6290-5d86-b376-ce0eb70b3fe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336705} {:answer/id #uuid "51a463d3-0093-55d7-a2ee-8312594e1a86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336780} {:answer/id #uuid "87045c5e-f1ca-5e3e-850f-ac86db303947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337253} {:answer/id #uuid "9ea3d299-fb71-5298-b534-c642be602e95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "485ed612-6290-5d86-b376-ce0eb70b3fe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336705} :answer-field/id #uuid "1eab19eb-a5c0-56fa-880c-7541dcfdcaf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2290a5a5-423d-532e-a1cb-06c374bf7c6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-72268" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}A,$ where \n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y(1-0)\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y & =-2[\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =2-\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339090 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2905b3c7-5120-5928-8659-ab7cf49215cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336415} {:answer/id #uuid "9c9f02de-6945-5986-9bd6-77c4fda53a38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337447} {:answer/id #uuid "c4c17bce-f321-5a5a-b8a2-e2f57aa0f0a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337781} {:answer/id #uuid "d1424795-2c4b-569b-984c-33e713fb9a2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337866} {:answer/id #uuid "f3baaf2a-856c-5b97-ba7e-56d8806079c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3baaf2a-856c-5b97-ba7e-56d8806079c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338162} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d342821-bbce-5fd1-b3bb-822b8446cf81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "83ace1c9-0a10-59aa-bb99-7a86a6dbfa17" :question/math-academy-id "q-72333" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{1/x}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{e}^{1/x}=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{1/x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]^{′}}{[{e}^{1/x}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}{{e}^{1/x}\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{{e}^{1/x}\\cdot {x}^{-2}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{x}{{e}^{1/x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339091 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21d77a93-af57-5abb-8f97-29ac2ca5d216" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336348} {:answer/id #uuid "67912699-7231-5c85-81fc-c774efe21db8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336956} {:answer/id #uuid "a24a093a-4a10-53da-8a31-41da6e3952e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337505} {:answer/id #uuid "d20fbb17-4105-57b9-9e4e-afbf7bfe0c89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337872} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ffad50-443d-59eb-ad44-76721b0d692c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a24a093a-4a10-53da-8a31-41da6e3952e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337505} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a96e9e3-c5a2-5aed-87c1-a11f5fde3b60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "32f3c869-0ed2-55fc-8f3f-7b188384b842" :question/math-academy-id "q-72335" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)}=$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to 0}{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}]}^{′}}{{[\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}\\left(-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}\\cancel{\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}}{\\cancel{\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}} \\\\  & =4{lim}_{x\\to 0}{e}^{2/{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339092 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dd6dd70-5f78-54b6-ac97-950f2f384aef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336473} {:answer/id #uuid "4e08260e-b4f6-5c7a-ad2d-8294f523a3ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336757} {:answer/id #uuid "5b538add-4209-5721-8b56-652729cc391f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336862} {:answer/id #uuid "7d7564a4-f738-5faf-a2c3-bdd625a0ef99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337163} {:answer/id #uuid "bb83b45c-700e-5a49-955e-7ea35708e865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e08260e-b4f6-5c7a-ad2d-8294f523a3ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336757} :answer-field/id #uuid "64250102-7382-5959-9fea-4e5f4501809d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d0dae206-0030-50d7-a470-3e9d541986a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-72336" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}]}^{′}}{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}}\\right)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{-3}}{4{x}^{-5}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339093 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d068af1-5717-50f9-98dd-688a202e224e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336300} {:answer/id #uuid "9776818d-6d25-54e4-a20d-e79199cc2aad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337414} {:answer/id #uuid "ab2a4700-b08b-5628-83cf-d16c711a2e5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337578} {:answer/id #uuid "c44a2cc7-2ec0-5efa-8bda-75dbe3460520" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337777} {:answer/id #uuid "c6c6f9fc-2923-5efc-a066-acf8386c9d66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d068af1-5717-50f9-98dd-688a202e224e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336300} :answer-field/id #uuid "5219204f-5acb-519a-b83e-7a3a43278127" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c9a1b2b-318e-5496-aa9a-d554d802f8d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-72338" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}\\right)}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}+8)}=$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}+8)=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}\\right)}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}+8)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}]}^{′}}{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}+8]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{7}}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}}\\right)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{6{x}^{-7}}{4{x}^{-5}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339100 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "181818f9-ee5d-570b-8805-3453bec6da26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{(1+{x}^{5})^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336257} {:answer/id #uuid "324b72d3-41e0-5026-af58-5b34dc0551ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336509} {:answer/id #uuid "601c5f25-3385-59f3-b9ec-6f4783410687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336901} {:answer/id #uuid "d13e9d9a-a682-5a93-b274-9657dd12c702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337864} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d6ab9a-d942-55d2-8674-766bbfa15318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{(1+{x}^{5})^{3}}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "324b72d3-41e0-5026-af58-5b34dc0551ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336509} :answer-field/id #uuid "1287170e-aae1-5d7e-9ef3-ec229bb6713f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "94a2c36c-7f20-5b89-950f-742f856d9127" :question/math-academy-id "q-72382" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{5{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $5{x}^{4}$ is the derivative of the quantity in the radical. So we make a substitution\n\n$$\nu=1+{x}^{5}.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =5{x}^{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=5{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand rewriting our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}}\\,5{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{1/2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral using the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{{u}^{1/2}}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =2{u}^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{u}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we substitute $u=1+{x}^{5}$ back in the expression. So the final result is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339111 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a0d85b8-3324-57f7-a467-f401be804083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(5x-7)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336578} {:answer/id #uuid "75e6122b-2104-52f4-b814-dae601247c06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{(5x-7)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337093} {:answer/id #uuid "8b91db05-c595-568e-b4ad-f97b5c3c6882" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{(5x-7)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337295} {:answer/id #uuid "aaed332c-7186-53b8-802e-6557581b75af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{(5x-7)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337573} {:answer/id #uuid "d9ae619c-f25b-53f5-8b0f-30595a2ff518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{(5x-7)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337926}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a0d85b8-3324-57f7-a467-f401be804083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(5x-7)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336578} :answer-field/id #uuid "eda4e982-7475-54c5-abfe-c6e946514b7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5692ce6-f75c-5a26-9487-0e066184decc" :question/math-academy-id "q-72429" :question/problem "$\\int -\\frac{15}{(5x-7)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int -15(5x-7)^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=5x-7.$Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -15(5x-7)^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -15{u}^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3\\int {u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right){u}^{-3}+C \\\\  & ={u}^{-3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(5x-7)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{(5x-7)^{3}}+C] & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{(5x-7)^{3}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(5x-7)^{-3}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (5x-7)^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5x-7)+0 \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (5x-7)^{-4}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{(5x-7)^{4}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339114 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38d2d1aa-0997-5cb2-b300-dfa2f96f8aec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336562} {:answer/id #uuid "59994cc9-84e6-5694-98fc-4ad89531a594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336850} {:answer/id #uuid "6dc5aa96-1f1c-55d1-b39a-b58513aa41ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337005} {:answer/id #uuid "bb7279db-0a2f-53c8-96ac-73280efb6211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337695} {:answer/id #uuid "c1a55623-531d-5deb-9228-f91988939757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59994cc9-84e6-5694-98fc-4ad89531a594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336850} :answer-field/id #uuid "89e15c6a-7b48-57d6-82fa-4853612f7217" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de671a41-8eec-5f15-a765-d0b4005272ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-7244" :question/problem "![](images/8b/8bde9a87a568654933ce9996f23605ea1219e2ff8416d9d5097971d9b6ef6087.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=k{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}+c.$ Find the value of $k-c.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-1.$ Hence, $c=-1.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,4),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=4$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}-1 & =y \\\\ k{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{0}-1 & =4 \\\\ k-1 & =4 \\\\ k & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk-c=5-(-1)=6.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=5{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339117 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07b76063-8fda-596f-8106-cbb57f2ef69a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5-2x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336093} {:answer/id #uuid "86f6a2e9-4d29-5e23-9617-09660280aaa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5-2x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337252} {:answer/id #uuid "b4ead48b-c59a-570e-a7de-39bcbc22b8fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5-2x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337638} {:answer/id #uuid "bec88b66-0b15-559a-8f7c-3341c51c2ee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5-2x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337729} {:answer/id #uuid "f6322442-f9d7-5f7c-ac2d-896d214f1436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5-2x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bec88b66-0b15-559a-8f7c-3341c51c2ee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5-2x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337729} :answer-field/id #uuid "209f233a-25a9-55a5-ab33-40122ec3bf02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2d475585-492e-5142-bfa4-12e97136e061" :question/math-academy-id "q-72446" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{5-2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." :question/worked-solution "First, let $u=5-2x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{5-2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5-2x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ for another arbitrary constant $K,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5-2x|+C & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5-2x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5-2x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5-2x|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{5-2x}\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5-2x|).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339121 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b2f1c83-8e64-5c5d-a7c8-61fec0e2b212" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336438} {:answer/id #uuid "5ae8a4ab-e8dd-5c9e-8034-034f85b09221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336858} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9b1b13-e0bb-5ca4-9acc-1057a08b5578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337167} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d09915-1432-5fb6-8119-db2c86b226d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337770} {:answer/id #uuid "ded27ff2-fbdc-54ae-a21b-ad7fdc344c0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ae8a4ab-e8dd-5c9e-8034-034f85b09221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336858} :answer-field/id #uuid "303db511-661b-5dcd-ac91-ae15b1d84c40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7bd82a27-9501-5619-90c8-a56e5e1d1b92" :question/math-academy-id "q-7246" :question/problem "![](images/f9/f960cca1134d756cded381a2236dd427efb928b1081254173e4c6903b6de150e.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x}.$ What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the graph of $y=k{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x},$ we take the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x}$ and stretch it vertically by a scale factor of  $k.$ Under the action of this transformation, the $y$-intercept is mapped from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k).$\n\nComparing the point $(0,1.5)$ on the graph to $(0,k),$ we see that we must have $k=1.5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339123 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e59935f-f93b-599b-9816-d15f61104595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{({x}^{2}-5)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336167} {:answer/id #uuid "1ccce204-ca7a-5eee-bc02-3fcc7e16e632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{({x}^{2}-5)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336299} {:answer/id #uuid "4b5b5f53-8881-5e03-8cde-ddfcc41895a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{({x}^{2}-5)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336732} {:answer/id #uuid "75e7362c-a6a5-5145-bf5d-1527db0f317d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{({x}^{2}-5)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337095} {:answer/id #uuid "7bc5cfb0-4b70-528a-a0e4-e88e561188b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{({x}^{2}-5)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75e7362c-a6a5-5145-bf5d-1527db0f317d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{({x}^{2}-5)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337095} :answer-field/id #uuid "80b5679a-f4f7-56bb-9b5b-253ee5665aec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de70203f-dd05-5f0d-9800-283717012618" :question/math-academy-id "q-72467" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-5}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the $x$ that's outside the square root isn't quite the derivative of $({x}^{2}-5).$ But the derivative is $2x,$ so we're only out by a constant factor.\n\nSo we make the substitution\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}-5.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can now write our integral in terms of $u,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-5}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve the integral in terms of $u$ and then write the final result in terms of $x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{3/2}}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{({x}^{2}-5)^{3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339128 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52c654bc-04c8-5012-92ba-be6dc4f3b157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{x}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336794} {:answer/id #uuid "618b5b65-bb8a-509b-8c37-05d672d232ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{x}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336910} {:answer/id #uuid "badd5577-56d9-588f-8ff5-f790b17df424" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337690} {:answer/id #uuid "c6475fa7-8b43-572a-bb99-293059034eed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337790} {:answer/id #uuid "ee1c4b7a-65db-5fce-84b6-561dd4ce2a2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{x}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338114}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee1c4b7a-65db-5fce-84b6-561dd4ce2a2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{x}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338114} :answer-field/id #uuid "e131a286-e66c-5d05-8d91-ddad9003ccc4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8bad530c-24c4-5ec2-8bc0-ed7cad8940a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-7252" :question/problem "![](images/f7/f7cb93e2aa4cede2739d43fc33fc01755b5848465d46fed764eef87a1f9d3df9.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{x}$ is $y=0.$\n\nThe vertical translation $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$ Therefore, $c=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339133 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "278a2661-eb4a-5ccf-94a9-e3323a7179af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336401} {:answer/id #uuid "48626940-2e9b-50a8-b80b-89ae33b65bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336706} {:answer/id #uuid "58330d9f-0dc5-5caf-af58-b2492b03a77b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336841} {:answer/id #uuid "5cf00bf3-2ab1-5fa1-9270-4bbc6255086b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336873} {:answer/id #uuid "da7de62a-9590-5e45-a089-249538a33439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da7de62a-9590-5e45-a089-249538a33439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337933} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c0ecdcc-f4de-5d40-9f9e-b7dcad3e1074" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "52a28619-4fbc-5936-812f-159a07cf7366" :question/math-academy-id "q-7255" :question/problem "![](images/df/df745423b3c91cddbfeb4a6414b17d69ee075ed2d02908f1e97aaecdbbf74679.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}$ is $y=0.$\n\nThe vertical translation $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-1.$ Therefore, $c=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341836 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e1988b7-4bc8-5bda-86c5-639e0552dedd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-3y=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339683} {:answer/id #uuid "51dac7ff-2d97-5661-b543-ad769758a707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339880} {:answer/id #uuid "6d11b816-6f21-575c-a776-01785873cb84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-3y=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340089} {:answer/id #uuid "803a7a88-7553-50e1-862d-0b786cfc737c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3y=13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340279} {:answer/id #uuid "af6755f9-b09b-562d-a589-5067ac2ec549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3y=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "803a7a88-7553-50e1-862d-0b786cfc737c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3y=13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340279} :answer-field/id #uuid "58e7ea68-a247-56ce-a08d-252af6a5df70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ad191a02-5856-5306-8980-ab9514c55d3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-72571" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(5,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(1,5)$ and $(-3,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(1,5)$ and $(-3,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-5}{-3-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6}{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(5,1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-5) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{10}{3} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{13}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we want to write the equation in standard form. So, we will bring the $x$ term to the left-hand side and multiply both sides of the equation by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}x+y & =\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ 3\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}x+y) & =3\\cdot \\frac{13}{3} \\\\ 2x+3y & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341840 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "424f1750-2255-5cd3-97f1-bff8ae1f779e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-5{x}^{5}|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339710} {:answer/id #uuid "64a24bd8-02d1-5da1-875a-ff8313d79449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5(2-5{x}^{5})^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340018} {:answer/id #uuid "cd66158f-066f-573b-a023-86fcc42573cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2(2-5{x}^{5})^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340950} {:answer/id #uuid "d40a6f74-0d5f-5192-b1f2-24adfd672a83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-5{x}^{5}|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341003} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c87e78-2256-558f-a463-3af7ee7e05a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-5{x}^{5}|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1c87e78-2256-558f-a463-3af7ee7e05a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-5{x}^{5}|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341259} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4f33047-2f0b-50bf-84ac-e403b1fabf2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "958efc2b-d91b-53e5-b712-1690bc1b7068" :question/math-academy-id "q-72605" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{10{x}^{4}}{2-5{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=2-5{x}^{5}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-25{x}^{4}\\,⟹\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{25}\\right)\\text{d}u={x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand we can solve the resulting integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{10{x}^{4}}{2-5{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\int \\frac{1}{2-5{x}^{5}}\\cdot {x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{25}\\right)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{25}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-5{x}^{5}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341860 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0132169e-f42a-5220-a69a-7c30d86438b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339140} {:answer/id #uuid "498cc57f-bffc-5f3f-9c58-3e208410fcbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339789} {:answer/id #uuid "636b5362-e589-54d7-8f67-2e414ac6b803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340012} {:answer/id #uuid "9aa8860b-a34f-5f23-92ee-cc1252701150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340524} {:answer/id #uuid "b9fdf63e-5df8-585b-b8a4-157f959f9d5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9fdf63e-5df8-585b-b8a4-157f959f9d5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340794} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d871a90-bd12-59f5-a9f9-fc4adc47aeff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47c4e34b-d3a4-5786-b909-c2df73d6cb32" :question/math-academy-id "q-72773" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$  what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](images/00/00d540b1f6412e4af2617452fbf0c58750d01e844d89d0d92cfacc0cb3e45e32.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{6}=\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341861 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "058faef1-3b46-5838-ad00-2a1c1929a8c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={48.6}^{∘},{135.7}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339171} {:answer/id #uuid "0d168ebd-5cd5-5402-9c4a-cadebb5eaac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={48.6}^{∘},{131.4}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339248} {:answer/id #uuid "432e19aa-9f5a-54b6-9308-f292c773c731" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={50.1}^{∘},{131.4}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339718} {:answer/id #uuid "ba53e46c-4638-5d2b-b081-5cddd5d6e00a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={45.9}^{∘},{131.4}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340798} {:answer/id #uuid "dfafa5d1-da3c-5e0a-87fa-2782023d8901" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={48.6}^{∘},{139.1}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d168ebd-5cd5-5402-9c4a-cadebb5eaac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={48.6}^{∘},{131.4}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339248} :answer-field/id #uuid "161f2d52-b604-57f6-a5d1-a33c34ad324b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e26e2e63-b096-5a84-a4a3-bdfd754248d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-72779" :question/problem "Calculate, in degrees, rounded to $1$ decimal place, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{3}{4}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)=48.590\\,377{…}^{∘}≈48.590\\,{4}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $48.590\\,{4}^{∘}$ lies inside the range, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=48.590\\,{4}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=48.590\\,{4}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=48.590\\,{4}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/48/4864e26ba05324041220dff99d1929b4c280b77456295d00d13d246279b4e628.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-48.590\\,{4}^{∘} \\\\  & =131.409\\,{6}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions (rounded to one decimal place) are $x={48.6}^{∘},{131.4}^{∘}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341867 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0728ac5b-7e79-576b-adec-4d30f1c3aaba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339190} {:answer/id #uuid "0a3b29d2-4a94-5fb1-b9d2-b53c7ee28b83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339213} {:answer/id #uuid "1c3f8e9c-6c9b-5885-bf56-6669a8495109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339369} {:answer/id #uuid "2c898d7f-7e81-5d2c-919e-d2c6184e87a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339514} {:answer/id #uuid "a084c4f1-65d6-5e7e-b06d-9f2e60c82d99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c898d7f-7e81-5d2c-919e-d2c6184e87a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339514} :answer-field/id #uuid "4100e90b-9d4e-566a-8305-372a98604ef5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa1432b6-ebd7-517d-b079-d328cbf0fa84" :question/math-academy-id "q-72831" :question/problem "![](images/23/23b4f6296c9e840ad90b8235fa66f17e767c61a6cfeac5bc9566fd1fbd35b004.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the line $y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.$ Find, in radians, the $x$-coordinate of the point $A.$\n\n$$\n\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The $y$-values of both functions are the same at the intersection point. Equating the two equations gives:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\n Now, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis value lies outside $\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ To get a value that's inside the required domain, we add $\\pi $ to the principal value. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\pi +{x}_{1}=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ ."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341868 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49ec8ef0-a2aa-59d0-b586-aee4023d0aba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339793} {:answer/id #uuid "8134019b-88f5-51c3-bcd5-4a61594e691e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340289} {:answer/id #uuid "ac36a2b3-d852-50d8-89c7-cf913a281e3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340699} {:answer/id #uuid "f6bf8e09-84a2-5c1e-885f-53546e4df408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341311} {:answer/id #uuid "fa4cc8a3-5728-5583-82c6-0f49623ab642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341339}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac36a2b3-d852-50d8-89c7-cf913a281e3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340699} :answer-field/id #uuid "d37cc0bf-e7ca-5908-bdb0-a8516539f248" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22367997-0344-5325-8db1-e55756e2e128" :question/math-academy-id "q-72833" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aae559883c97bbd19321d9baeb26fe15e47cced5b072e12100c42aa579c9d02d.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the line $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.$ Find, in radians, the $x$-coordinate of the point $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The $y$-values of both functions are the same at the intersection point. Equating the two functions gives:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\n Now, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies inside the required domain, and there is only one point of intersection, we conclude that the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point $A$ is $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341869 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00d5e468-bffd-5584-a784-ecfb449532a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339136} {:answer/id #uuid "1d9fde13-58db-5370-bd76-bbe16a84773a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339388} {:answer/id #uuid "8d60791b-0a87-5cc9-ba85-a275aea14130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340397} {:answer/id #uuid "c37561d1-0002-57fd-b8ac-e023317c6504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340866} {:answer/id #uuid "c4148f61-7aa1-5439-b757-b2b6cfede3f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c37561d1-0002-57fd-b8ac-e023317c6504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340866} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6880e55-2f26-507b-b040-ba6b2bd724bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c47beb4-7d28-5eec-96a0-29c080da4a45" :question/math-academy-id "q-72835" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right) & =2.300\\,523…≈2.301\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2.301$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=2.301.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -2.301 & ≈0.841.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.841.$\n\n![](images/d0/d02a0250df0f0e6c389594ea0e1b119d9f4c63c353553d56c0e865f487862284.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.841 \\\\  & ≈3.983\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}≈2.301\\cdot 3.983≈9\n$$\n\nto the nearest integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341870 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "416a58ae-ce76-5e02-96f0-d7597149753a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339708} {:answer/id #uuid "6db8cad2-a957-58f3-ad26-db45b399e754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340098} {:answer/id #uuid "a1cd8970-9cb3-5318-9504-b8c8a617dc91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340596} {:answer/id #uuid "b6c00063-961c-57de-9996-aa7c34c1b08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340780} {:answer/id #uuid "ff350533-a65b-5d8c-9636-be091780422d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff350533-a65b-5d8c-9636-be091780422d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341376} :answer-field/id #uuid "90108bbe-e3f9-552f-9a17-4a20b275ed27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341634}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40af3b43-8968-5b55-af69-0a7608e19327" :question/math-academy-id "q-72838" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{4}{7}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4}{7}\\right)=0.962\\,550…≈0.962\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0.962\\,6$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.962\\,6.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.962\\,6.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.962\\,6.$\n\n![](images/b2/b2cd7e76526632d8507ba484246a560829882e7f10d9814f01593f7a48d9a2ac.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -0.962\\,6 \\\\  & =5.320\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}≈0.962\\,6\\cdot 5.320\\,6≈5.1\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341872 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55edaa2a-1154-52d8-9530-260261395249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339915} {:answer/id #uuid "68873d2d-aa7a-5baf-8be5-8fba31e19518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340059} {:answer/id #uuid "83293cdb-be49-5f68-8bac-1c64d296a1a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340308} {:answer/id #uuid "d896e395-515a-5bc0-bbe7-fc3b0614ef52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341052} {:answer/id #uuid "e1777609-516c-552a-bb28-eaa56e9eb7af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55edaa2a-1154-52d8-9530-260261395249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339915} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a113223-08c8-5467-aa70-c9c96110b9bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3b3398cc-8758-5d62-8796-0ec13a13ea7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-72840" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{4}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)=1.318\\,116…≈1.318\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1.318$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=1.318.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=1.318.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.318.$\n\n![](images/87/8797bd2b1558e094b72d891fd344b356e5d579e4549b788bdd0507de23f171c7.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -1.318 \\\\  & =4.965\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}≈1.318\\cdot 4.965≈7\n$$\n\nto the nearest integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341873 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "145badac-b27b-5e69-a317-ede02a7c4f7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339310} {:answer/id #uuid "4f6baa6f-2d68-5d47-8c11-d30883e928ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339851} {:answer/id #uuid "567145f7-185d-5f68-84f3-337783ccf578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339918} {:answer/id #uuid "7b781b9d-9ba3-5c98-a2bf-5dffb62b656f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340234} {:answer/id #uuid "86616c3c-5567-54ea-aeed-808f226c030c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86616c3c-5567-54ea-aeed-808f226c030c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340334} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4071a95-7784-5e52-b3d6-d1995e73002a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9975124e-84e0-58f3-a162-bfccabebefd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-72841" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{5}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{2}{5}\\right) & =1.982\\,313…≈1.982\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1.982\\,3$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=1.982\\,3.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -1.982\\,3 & ≈1.159\\,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.159\\,3.$\n\n![](images/25/250adbaae0378e3f42ab4340479bb4590aa7f39a1094ea39010de3d5758b4e3e.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +1.159\\,3 \\\\  & ≈4.300\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}≈1.982\\,3\\cdot 4.300\\,9≈8.5\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341882 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b996720-fe76-527e-b639-dd4409e18034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}t-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339960} {:answer/id #uuid "a98de0a0-9fd5-5afa-9d38-9aabd800ddfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340672} {:answer/id #uuid "ccc2c008-aa1a-527d-8c07-3caeedf8eaae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340940} {:answer/id #uuid "eaef0bad-552a-5d4e-85b6-f49a9ab354a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341203} {:answer/id #uuid "fd027a0e-52e6-59da-a871-423b0692b4d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}-\\frac{5}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a98de0a0-9fd5-5afa-9d38-9aabd800ddfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340672} :answer-field/id #uuid "3644f722-09d5-5e49-bbfd-14847dd6a5d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f823e7c2-6ed4-59db-a566-84d27827d618" :question/math-academy-id "q-7287" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}-t}{4},$ where $t>0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=-\\frac{1}{6}$ when $t=2,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}-t}{4}\\right)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $x(2)=-\\frac{1}{6}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{6} & =\\frac{1}{12}(2)^{3}-\\frac{1}{8}(2)^{2}+C \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6} & =\\frac{1}{6}+C \\\\ C & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341893 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "368d6f09-f86f-5a1f-934f-edb2ada2ecdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,\\frac{1}{2},1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339616} {:answer/id #uuid "4016e3b4-303c-5e7d-bc9c-f626ddc0e67a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,-1,\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339702} {:answer/id #uuid "5a087a29-e907-5bbd-8024-d1c0e3d6066a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339946} {:answer/id #uuid "7f49b16e-bca9-5992-802b-f9800e9b025a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\frac{1}{2},-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340272} {:answer/id #uuid "b28c83dc-7bea-5c71-a4cc-8b4d73503adb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2},-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a087a29-e907-5bbd-8024-d1c0e3d6066a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339946} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d72ac37-7d4a-57f8-a026-426aa0ba98f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6577a967-44f3-50e6-9603-cd6d17cab94e" :question/math-academy-id "q-72983" :question/problem "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x$, $y$, and $z.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y+z &=-1 \\\\ y+z &=1 \\\\ 2z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that\n \n\n$$\n2z=1\\,⟹\\,z=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=1\\,⟹\\,y=1-\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=y=\\frac{1}{2}$  into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n2x+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-1\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}(-1-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-1$, $y=\\frac{1}{2}$, and $z=\\frac{1}{2}.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨-1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩.$"}]
